/* AI brain Q&A pack 10: human body & health */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["what are the body systems | what are the organ systems | list the systems of the human body | how many body systems are there", "Your body has about **11 major organ systems** that work together:\n- Skeletal and muscular (support and movement)\n- Nervous (control and senses)\n- Circulatory and respiratory (blood and breathing)\n- Digestive and urinary (food and waste)\n- Immune and lymphatic (defense)\n- Endocrine (hormones)\n- Skin (integumentary) and reproductive"],
  ["what is an organ | what counts as an organ | define organ in the body", "An **organ** is a body part made of different tissues working together to do a specific job, like the heart pumping blood or the stomach breaking down food. Organs team up to form organ systems."],
  ["what is tissue | what are tissues in the body | types of tissue", "**Tissue** is a group of similar cells that work together. The four main types are **muscle** tissue (moves things), **nerve** tissue (sends signals), **epithelial** tissue (covers and lines surfaces) and **connective** tissue (supports and holds things together, like bone and blood)."],
  ["what is the largest internal organ | biggest organ inside the body | what is the heaviest organ inside you", "The **liver** is the largest organ inside your body, weighing about 1.5 kg (3 pounds) in adults. The skin is bigger overall, but it's on the outside."],
  ["can the liver grow back | can your liver regenerate | does the liver heal itself", "Yes! The **liver can regrow** itself. Even if a large part is removed, the remaining piece can grow back to nearly full size within a few months, which is why people can donate part of their liver."],
  ["why do we need a liver | what jobs does the liver have | liver function", "The **liver** does hundreds of jobs: it filters toxins out of your blood, makes bile to help digest fats, stores energy and vitamins, makes proteins that help blood clot, and helps control blood sugar."],
  ["what does the skeleton do | what is the skeletal system | why do we have a skeleton | why do we need bones", "Your **skeleton** holds you up, gives your body shape, protects soft organs (your skull guards your brain and your ribs guard your heart and lungs), lets you move with your muscles, stores minerals like calcium, and makes blood cells inside the bone marrow."],
  ["what are bones made of | what is inside a bone | are bones solid", "Bones are made of **collagen** (a bendy protein) hardened with minerals like **calcium and phosphate**. They have a hard outer layer, a spongy honeycomb layer inside, and many contain soft **bone marrow** in the middle. That design makes them strong but light."],
  ["are bones alive | are bones living tissue | do bones grow", "Yes, **bones are alive**! They have blood vessels, nerves and living cells that constantly break down old bone and build new bone. That's how they grow, get stronger with exercise and heal when broken."],
  ["what is bone marrow | what does bone marrow do | where is bone marrow", "**Bone marrow** is soft, spongy tissue inside many bones. Red marrow makes red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets, millions every second. Yellow marrow mostly stores fat."],
  ["how do broken bones heal | how does a bone fix itself | how long does a broken bone take to heal", "When a bone breaks, a blood clot forms, then the body builds a soft bridge of new tissue called a **callus**, which slowly hardens into new bone. A cast or splint holds it still so it heals straight. Most breaks take about **6 to 8 weeks**, often faster in kids. Always see a doctor if you think a bone is broken."],
  ["what is a joint | what are joints | what are the types of joints", "A **joint** is where two bones meet. Types include:\n- **Hinge** joints (knees, elbows) that bend one way\n- **Ball-and-socket** joints (hips, shoulders) that move in many directions\n- **Pivot** joints (neck) that rotate\n- **Fixed** joints (skull) that don't move"],
  ["what is cartilage | what is cartilage made of | where do we have cartilage", "**Cartilage** is a tough, flexible tissue that cushions joints so bones don't grind together. It also shapes your **nose and ears**. Wiggle the tip of your nose: that's cartilage, not bone!"],
  ["what is a ligament | what do ligaments do | ligament vs tendon | difference between tendons and ligaments", "**Ligaments** connect **bone to bone** and keep joints stable. **Tendons** connect **muscle to bone** so that when a muscle pulls, the bone moves."],
  ["what is the achilles tendon | what is the strongest tendon | where is the achilles", "The **Achilles tendon** runs down the back of your ankle and connects your calf muscles to your heel bone. It's the **largest and strongest tendon** in the body and helps you walk, run and jump."],
  ["what is the spine | what are vertebrae | how many bones are in the spine | what is the backbone", "Your **spine** (backbone) is a stack of **33 small bones called vertebrae** in babies; some fuse together, leaving about 24 movable ones in adults. Soft discs between them act like shock absorbers, and the spine protects your **spinal cord**."],
  ["how many ribs do humans have | how many ribs do we have | do men and women have the same number of ribs", "Most people have **24 ribs**, 12 pairs, and men and women have the same number. They form a cage that protects your heart and lungs and moves when you breathe."],
  ["what is the skull | how many bones are in the skull | what does the skull protect", "The **skull** protects your brain and shapes your face. It's made of **22 bones**, and all except the lower jaw are locked together by wavy joints called sutures."],
  ["why do babies have a soft spot | what is the soft spot on a baby's head | what is a fontanelle", "Babies have **soft spots (fontanelles)** where their skull bones haven't joined yet. This lets the head squeeze safely during birth and gives the brain room to grow. They close up during the first couple of years."],
  ["what is the funny bone | why does hitting your funny bone hurt | is the funny bone a bone", "The **funny bone isn't a bone**: it's the **ulnar nerve**, which runs close to the skin at your elbow. Bumping it squeezes the nerve and sends a weird tingly zap down your arm to your pinky."],
  ["why do knuckles crack | what is the popping sound when you crack your knuckles | does cracking knuckles cause arthritis", "The pop comes from **gas bubbles** forming in the fluid inside your joint when you stretch it. Studies have found **no link to arthritis**, but if cracking a joint hurts or swells, tell an adult and see a doctor."],
  ["are teeth bones | is a tooth a bone | are teeth part of the skeleton", "**Teeth are not bones**, even though both contain calcium. Teeth can't repair themselves the way bones do, and their outer enamel is the **hardest substance in your body**."],
  ["what are growth plates | how do bones get longer | why do kids grow taller", "**Growth plates** are areas of cartilage near the ends of long bones in kids and teens. New bone forms there, making bones longer. After puberty they harden into solid bone, and you stop getting taller."],
  ["what is the kneecap | what does the kneecap do | what is the patella", "Your **kneecap (patella)** is a small bone in front of your knee. It protects the joint and helps your thigh muscles straighten your leg with more power. Babies' kneecaps are mostly soft cartilage at first."],
  ["what is the only bone not connected to another bone | what is the hyoid bone | floating bone in the body", "The **hyoid bone** in your neck is the only bone not attached to any other bone. It's held by muscles and ligaments and helps you **swallow and speak**."],
  ["what is the strongest bone in the body | strongest bone | which bone is the hardest to break", "The **femur (thigh bone)** is the strongest bone in your body. It can support many times your body weight. It's also the longest bone."],
  ["what is osteoporosis | why do old people's bones break easily | what makes bones weak", "**Osteoporosis** is when bones become thin, weak and break more easily, mostly in older adults. Building strong bones while you're young, with **calcium, vitamin D and exercise** like running and jumping, helps protect them for life."],
  ["how do i get strong bones | how to make bones stronger | what is good for bones", "To build strong bones:\n- Eat **calcium** (milk, yogurt, cheese, fortified plant milk, tofu, leafy greens)\n- Get **vitamin D** (sunlight, eggs, fish, fortified foods)\n- Do **weight-bearing exercise** like running, jumping and dancing\n- Avoid lots of soda and get plenty of sleep"],
  ["what is the collarbone | what is the clavicle | why do collarbones break easily", "The **collarbone (clavicle)** connects your arm to your body across the top of your chest. It's one of the most commonly broken bones because it absorbs the force when people fall on an outstretched arm."],
  ["what is the pelvis | what are the hip bones | what does the pelvis do", "The **pelvis** is a bowl-shaped ring of bones at the base of your spine. It connects your spine to your legs, supports your body weight, and protects organs like your bladder."],
  ["what is an x-ray | how do x-rays work | why do bones show up on x-rays", "An **X-ray** sends a tiny, safe burst of radiation through your body. Dense bones absorb more of it and show up **white**, while softer tissues let more through and look darker. Doctors use X-rays to spot broken bones and check teeth."],
  ["what is the muscular system | what do muscles do for the body | why do we need muscles", "The **muscular system** lets you move, keeps your posture upright, pumps blood (heart muscle), moves food through your gut and even makes heat to keep you warm. Muscles work by **contracting**, which means pulling, never pushing."],
  ["what are the three types of muscle | types of muscles | kinds of muscle tissue", "There are **3 types of muscle**:\n- **Skeletal** muscle: attached to bones, moves when you decide to\n- **Smooth** muscle: in organs like your stomach and blood vessels, works automatically\n- **Cardiac** muscle: found only in the heart, never gets tired"],
  ["what are voluntary and involuntary muscles | voluntary vs involuntary muscle | which muscles do we control", "**Voluntary** muscles (skeletal muscles) are the ones you control, like moving your arm. **Involuntary** muscles (smooth and heart muscle) work automatically, like your heart beating and your stomach digesting, without you thinking about them."],
  ["why do muscles work in pairs | biceps and triceps | how do muscles move bones", "Muscles can only **pull**, not push, so they work in **pairs**. To bend your elbow, your **biceps** contracts while your **triceps** relaxes. To straighten it, the triceps contracts and the biceps relaxes."],
  ["how do muscles get stronger | how do muscles grow | how do you build muscle", "When you exercise hard, muscle fibers get tiny bits of damage. While you rest, your body **repairs them a little bigger and stronger**. Protein, sleep and regular practice help muscles grow."],
  ["why are my muscles sore after exercise | why do muscles hurt the day after working out | what is muscle soreness", "Soreness a day or two after exercise is called **DOMS (delayed onset muscle soreness)**. It comes from tiny strains in muscle fibers as they adapt to new work, and it usually fades in **2 to 3 days**. Gentle movement, water and rest help. Sharp pain or swelling is different: tell an adult."],
  ["why do we get muscle cramps | what is a cramp | why do i get leg cramps | what causes cramps", "A **cramp** is when a muscle suddenly tightens and won't relax. They can happen from tired muscles, **not drinking enough water**, or staying in one position too long. Gently **stretching and massaging** the muscle usually helps. Frequent cramps are worth mentioning to a doctor."],
  ["what is a side stitch | why do i get a stitch when running | how to get rid of a side stitch", "A **side stitch** is a sharp pain under your ribs during exercise. Scientists aren't 100% sure of the cause, but it may be irritation of the lining around your belly. Slow down, **breathe deeply**, and press gently on the spot. Avoiding a big meal right before running can help."],
  ["what is the largest muscle in the body | biggest muscle | what is the gluteus maximus", "The **gluteus maximus** (your bottom muscle) is the largest muscle in your body. It keeps you upright and powers walking, running and climbing stairs."],
  ["what is the smallest muscle in the body | smallest muscle | what is the stapedius", "The **stapedius** in your middle ear is the smallest muscle, only about 1 mm long. It steadies the tiniest bone (the stirrup) to help protect your ear from very loud sounds."],
  ["what is the fastest muscle in the body | fastest muscle | how fast can you blink", "The muscles that **close your eyelids** are among the fastest in the body. A blink takes only about **a tenth of a second**."],
  ["is the tongue a muscle | is the tongue the strongest muscle | what is the tongue made of", "The **tongue** is a group of **8 muscles** that work together to help you talk, chew, taste and swallow. It's very flexible and tireless, but calling it the strongest muscle is a myth."],
  ["how many muscles does it take to smile | does it take more muscles to frown than smile", "Smiling and frowning each use **around 10 to 12 facial muscles**, depending on the expression. The popular claim that frowning takes way more muscles isn't really accurate, but smiling is still a great idea!"],
  ["what is the diaphragm | what muscle helps you breathe | what does the diaphragm do", "The **diaphragm** is a dome-shaped muscle under your lungs. When it **tightens and flattens**, your lungs fill with air; when it relaxes, air flows out. It's the main muscle for breathing."],
  ["why do my muscles shake when i'm tired | why do muscles tremble during exercise | why do my legs shake after working out", "When muscles get tired, the nerve signals controlling them become less smooth, and fibers start firing unevenly, causing **shaking**. It's usually normal after hard exercise. Rest, water and a snack help."],
  ["what is muscle memory | how does muscle memory work | why do skills become automatic", "**Muscle memory** isn't actually stored in muscles: it's your **brain** learning a movement so well through practice that it becomes automatic, like riding a bike or typing. Repetition builds stronger brain pathways."],
  ["what is a sprain | what is a strain | sprain vs strain | difference between a sprain and a strain", "A **sprain** is a stretched or torn **ligament** (like a twisted ankle). A **strain** is a stretched or torn **muscle or tendon**. For minor ones, people use **RICE**: Rest, Ice (wrapped in a cloth), Compression and Elevation. See a doctor if you can't put weight on it or it's very swollen."],
  ["central vs peripheral nervous system | what is the central nervous system | what is the peripheral nervous system | what are the parts of the nervous system", "The **nervous system** is your body's control and message network. The **central nervous system** (brain and spinal cord) makes decisions, and the **peripheral nervous system** (nerves throughout your body) carries messages to and from it."],
  ["how fast do nerve signals travel | how fast does the brain send messages | speed of nerve impulses", "The fastest nerve signals travel up to about **120 meters per second** (around 270 mph). That's why you can pull your hand off something hot almost instantly."],
  ["what is the spinal cord | what does the spinal cord do | why is the spinal cord important", "The **spinal cord** is a thick bundle of nerves running from your brain down your back, protected by your spine. It carries messages between your brain and body and handles some **reflexes** on its own."],
  ["what is a reflex | why do we have reflexes | what is a reflex action", "A **reflex** is an automatic reaction that happens without you thinking, like pulling your hand from a hot stove or blinking when something flies at your eye. The signal often goes through your **spinal cord** instead of waiting for your brain, which saves time and protects you."],
  ["why does my leg kick when the doctor taps my knee | what is the knee jerk reflex | knee reflex test", "That's the **knee-jerk reflex**. Tapping the tendon below your kneecap stretches your thigh muscle, and your spinal cord instantly tells it to contract, so your leg kicks. Doctors test it to check your nerves are working well."],
  ["what does the frontal lobe do | what are the lobes of the brain | what are the four lobes", "The brain's outer layer has **4 lobes**:\n- **Frontal**: planning, decisions, personality and movement\n- **Parietal**: touch, temperature and body position\n- **Temporal**: hearing, language and memory\n- **Occipital**: vision"],
  ["what does the brainstem do | what is the brain stem | what controls breathing and heartbeat", "The **brainstem** connects your brain to your spinal cord and controls automatic life functions like **breathing, heartbeat, blood pressure, swallowing and sleeping**, all without you thinking about them."],
  ["how much does the brain weigh | how big is the human brain | how heavy is your brain", "An adult brain weighs about **1.4 kg (3 pounds)** and is roughly the size of two fists together. It's soft, a bit like firm tofu, and is about **75% water**."],
  ["how much energy does the brain use | does thinking burn calories | why does the brain need so much energy", "Your brain is only about 2% of your body weight but uses around **20% of your energy**. It runs constantly, even when you're asleep, mostly on **glucose** (sugar) from food."],
  ["is the left brain right brain thing true | are people left brained or right brained | left brain vs right brain", "The idea that people are **left-brained (logical) or right-brained (creative) is a myth**. Some skills lean more on one side, like language often on the left, but healthy brains use **both sides together** all the time."],
  ["how does memory work | how does the brain remember things | where are memories stored", "Memories form when **brain cells strengthen their connections**. The **hippocampus** helps turn new experiences into long-term memories, which are then stored across many parts of the brain. Sleep, repetition and attention all help memories stick."],
  ["short term vs long term memory | what is short term memory | what is long term memory", "**Short-term memory** holds small bits of info for seconds to minutes, like a number you're about to type. **Long-term memory** stores things for days to a lifetime, like your friend's name or how to swim. Repeating and using information moves it into long-term memory."],
  ["why do we forget things | why do i forget stuff | why can't i remember things", "Forgetting is normal: your brain **filters out** information it doesn't use, so it can focus on what matters. Things you don't revisit fade, and stress or poor sleep makes remembering harder. Reviewing, sleeping well and connecting new facts to things you know all help."],
  ["what is a concussion | what happens when you hit your head hard | concussion symptoms", "A **concussion** is a brain injury caused by a hit or jolt to the head. Signs can include headache, dizziness, confusion, feeling sick or sleepy. Anyone who hits their head and feels off should **stop playing, tell an adult and see a doctor**. Get emergency help for passing out, repeated vomiting or confusion that gets worse."],
  ["why does my foot fall asleep | what are pins and needles | why do my legs go numb when i sit", "When you sit on your foot or lean on your arm, you **press on a nerve** and squeeze blood flow, so signals get interrupted and it goes numb. When you move, the nerve wakes up and sends a burst of tingly **pins and needles**. It goes away in a minute or two."],
  ["why do we feel pain | what is pain for | how does pain work", "**Pain** is your body's alarm system. Special nerve endings detect damage or danger and send signals to your brain, which makes you feel pain so you'll **stop and protect yourself**. It's unpleasant, but it keeps you safe."],
  ["can the brain feel pain | does the brain have pain receptors | why do headaches hurt if the brain can't feel pain", "The brain tissue itself has **no pain receptors**, so it can't feel pain directly. Headaches come from nerves in the **muscles, blood vessels and coverings** around the brain, head and neck."],
  ["what are neurotransmitters | how do neurons talk to each other | what is a synapse", "**Neurotransmitters** are chemical messengers that carry signals across the tiny gap between neurons, called a **synapse**. Examples include dopamine, serotonin and acetylcholine, which affect movement, mood, sleep and more."],
  ["what is dopamine | what does dopamine do | is dopamine the happy chemical", "**Dopamine** is a brain chemical involved in **motivation, reward and movement**. It's released when you look forward to something or achieve a goal, which helps you learn what's worth doing again."],
  ["what is serotonin | what does serotonin do", "**Serotonin** is a brain chemical that helps regulate **mood, sleep, appetite and digestion**. In fact, most of your body's serotonin is actually made in your gut!"],
  ["why can't you tickle yourself | why are people ticklish | what makes us ticklish", "Your brain (especially the **cerebellum**) predicts the feeling of your own movements and turns down the signal, so you can't really surprise yourself. When someone else tickles you, it's unpredictable, which makes you squirm and laugh."],
  ["what is deja vu | why do we get deja vu | why does something feel like it happened before", "**Déjà vu** is the strange feeling that you've experienced a moment before. Scientists think it's a little **memory hiccup**, when the brain's familiarity system fires even though the situation is new. It's common and harmless."],
  ["why do we get dizzy when we spin | why does spinning make you dizzy | why does the room spin after spinning", "Your inner ear has fluid-filled tubes that sense motion. When you spin, the fluid swirls. When you stop, the **fluid keeps moving** for a few seconds, so your brain thinks you're still spinning while your eyes say you're not, and you feel dizzy."],
  ["what is the hypothalamus | what does the hypothalamus do | what controls body temperature", "The **hypothalamus** is a tiny part of your brain that acts like a thermostat and control center. It manages **body temperature, hunger, thirst and sleep**, and tells the pituitary gland which hormones to release."],
  ["how many neurons are in the brain | how many brain cells do we have | how many connections in the brain", "The human brain has about **86 billion neurons**, plus many supporting cells. Each neuron can link to thousands of others, making **trillions of connections**."],
  ["what is the circulatory system | what does the circulatory system do | what is the cardiovascular system", "The **circulatory system** is your heart, blood and blood vessels. It delivers **oxygen and nutrients** to every cell and carries away waste like carbon dioxide."],
  ["what makes the heart beat | why does the heart beat on its own | what is the heart's pacemaker", "Your heart has its own built-in **natural pacemaker** called the **SA node**. It sends out tiny electrical signals that make the heart muscle squeeze in rhythm, about 100,000 times a day, without you thinking about it."],
  ["why does the heart make a lub dub sound | what is the heartbeat sound | what makes the sound of a heartbeat", "The **lub-dub** sound is made by your **heart valves snapping shut**. 'Lub' is the valves between the upper and lower chambers closing, and 'dub' is the valves leading out of the heart closing right after."],
  ["how many chambers does the heart have | what are the parts of the heart | what are atria and ventricles", "The heart has **4 chambers**. The two upper ones (**atria**) receive blood, and the two lower ones (**ventricles**) pump it out. The right side sends blood to the lungs to get oxygen, and the left side pumps oxygen-rich blood to the rest of the body."],
  ["what are heart valves | what do heart valves do", "**Heart valves** are four little flaps that act like one-way doors, keeping blood flowing in the right direction through your heart and stopping it from leaking backward."],
  ["how big is your heart | how big is the human heart | where is the heart located", "Your heart is about the **size of your fist** and sits in the middle of your chest, tilted slightly to the left, protected by your ribs and breastbone."],
  ["how much blood does the heart pump | how much blood does the heart pump in a day", "An adult heart pumps around **5 liters of blood per minute** at rest, which adds up to about **7,000 liters (roughly 2,000 gallons)** every day."],
  ["what are arteries veins and capillaries | difference between arteries and veins | types of blood vessels", "There are 3 kinds of blood vessels:\n- **Arteries** carry blood **away from** the heart\n- **Veins** carry blood **back to** the heart\n- **Capillaries** are tiny tubes connecting them, where oxygen and nutrients pass into your cells"],
  ["how long are all your blood vessels | how many miles of blood vessels are in the body", "If you laid all of an adult's blood vessels end to end, they'd stretch an estimated **100,000 km (about 60,000 miles)**, enough to wrap around Earth more than twice! Most of that length is tiny capillaries."],
  ["how long does it take blood to go around the body | how fast does blood circulate", "At rest, it takes only about **one minute** for blood to travel from your heart, around your body and back again."],
  ["what is blood pressure | what does blood pressure mean | what do blood pressure numbers mean", "**Blood pressure** is how hard your blood pushes against the walls of your arteries. It's written as two numbers, like 120/80: the top is the pressure when your heart beats, and the bottom is when it rests between beats. Doctors check it because very high pressure can strain the heart over time."],
  ["what is a pulse | how do i find my pulse | how to check your heart rate", "Your **pulse** is the throb of blood pushed through your arteries with each heartbeat. Press two fingers (not your thumb) gently on the inside of your wrist below the thumb, or on the side of your neck. Count the beats for 15 seconds and multiply by 4 to get beats per minute."],
  ["why does my heart beat faster when i exercise | why does your heart race when running", "When you exercise, your muscles need much more **oxygen and fuel**. Your heart beats faster and harder to pump more blood to them, and you breathe faster to bring in more oxygen."],
  ["why does my heart beat fast when i'm scared | why does the heart pound when nervous", "When you're scared or nervous, your body releases **adrenaline**, part of the fight-or-flight response. It makes your heart pump faster to get your muscles ready for action. Slow, deep breaths help it calm back down."],
  ["what are platelets | how does blood clot | how do cuts stop bleeding", "**Platelets** are tiny cell pieces in your blood. When you get a cut, they rush to the spot and stick together, and proteins form a mesh called **fibrin** that traps blood cells into a **clot**, plugging the leak."],
  ["what is a scab | why do scabs form | should you pick a scab", "A **scab** is a dried blood clot that acts like a natural bandage while new skin grows underneath. **Don't pick it**: that can let germs in, slow healing and leave a scar."],
  ["what is blood plasma | what is the liquid part of blood | what is plasma in blood", "**Plasma** is the pale yellow liquid part of blood, making up about **55%** of it. It's mostly water and carries nutrients, hormones, proteins and waste around the body."],
  ["why do bruises change color | what is a bruise | why do bruises turn green and yellow", "A **bruise** happens when tiny blood vessels under the skin break. It starts red or purple, then turns blue, **green and yellow** as your body breaks down the trapped blood. Most fade in about 2 weeks."],
  ["how long do red blood cells live | where are blood cells made | how often does the body make new blood", "**Red blood cells** live about **120 days** and are made in your bone marrow, millions every second. Old ones are recycled by your spleen and liver."],
  ["what is hemoglobin | what does hemoglobin do | what carries oxygen in blood", "**Hemoglobin** is an iron-containing protein in red blood cells that **grabs oxygen** in your lungs and drops it off around your body. It's also what makes blood red."],
  ["what is anemia | why would someone be anemic | what does low iron mean", "**Anemia** means your blood doesn't have enough healthy red blood cells or hemoglobin, so less oxygen reaches your body. It can make people tired, pale or short of breath. A common cause is low iron. Only a doctor can diagnose it with a blood test."],
  ["what is cholesterol | is cholesterol bad | good vs bad cholesterol", "**Cholesterol** is a waxy substance your body needs to make cells and hormones. **LDL** ('bad') can build up in arteries if there's too much, while **HDL** ('good') helps carry extra away. Eating fiber, fruits, vegetables and less saturated fat, plus exercise, helps keep it healthy."],
  ["how do i keep my heart healthy | heart healthy habits | how to have a healthy heart", "To keep your heart healthy:\n- Be active for at least an hour a day (kids) or 150 minutes a week (adults)\n- Eat fruits, vegetables, whole grains and less salt and sugar\n- Don't smoke or vape\n- Get enough sleep and manage stress"],
  ["what is a heart attack | what happens in a heart attack | signs of a heart attack", "A **heart attack** happens when blood flow to part of the heart muscle gets blocked. Common signs are chest pain or pressure, pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, shortness of breath and sweating. It's an **emergency: call your local emergency number right away**."],
  ["what is a stroke | signs of a stroke | what does fast mean for a stroke", "A **stroke** happens when blood flow to part of the brain is blocked or a vessel bursts. Remember **FAST**:\n- **F**ace drooping\n- **A**rm weakness\n- **S**peech trouble\n- **T**ime to call emergency services immediately"],
  ["what is the lymphatic system | what does the lymphatic system do | what is lymph", "The **lymphatic system** is a network of vessels and nodes that carries a clear fluid called **lymph**. It drains extra fluid from tissues back into your blood and helps your immune system catch germs."],
  ["what are lymph nodes | why do glands swell when you're sick | swollen glands in neck", "**Lymph nodes** are small bean-shaped filters packed with immune cells. When you're fighting an infection they can get **swollen and tender**, often in your neck, as they work hard. If swelling lasts more than a couple of weeks, see a doctor."],
  ["what does the spleen do | what is the spleen | can you live without a spleen", "The **spleen** sits on the upper left side of your belly. It filters blood, recycles old red blood cells, and helps fight infections. People **can live without one**, though they need to be a bit more careful about infections."],
  ["what is the respiratory system | what are the parts of the respiratory system | how does air get to your lungs", "The **respiratory system** brings oxygen in and takes carbon dioxide out. Air travels through your **nose or mouth**, down the **trachea (windpipe)**, into two **bronchi**, then smaller tubes, and finally into tiny air sacs called **alveoli** in your lungs."],
  ["what are alveoli | what are air sacs in the lungs | how does oxygen get into blood", "**Alveoli** are tiny balloon-like air sacs at the ends of your airways, and you have **hundreds of millions** of them. They're wrapped in capillaries, so **oxygen passes into your blood** and carbon dioxide passes out to be breathed away."],
  ["why do we need to breathe | what does oxygen do in the body | what do cells use oxygen for", "Your cells use **oxygen** to turn food into energy, a process called cellular respiration. Without oxygen, cells (especially brain cells) can't make enough energy to keep you alive, which is why we breathe all the time."],
  ["why do we breathe out carbon dioxide | where does carbon dioxide come from in the body", "When your cells use oxygen to release energy from food, they produce **carbon dioxide** as waste. Your blood carries it back to your lungs, and you breathe it out."],
  ["how many times do we breathe a day | how many breaths per minute | how many breaths in a day", "Adults at rest breathe about **12 to 20 times a minute**, which is roughly **20,000 breaths a day**. Kids breathe a bit faster."],
  ["what is the windpipe | what is the trachea | what does the trachea do", "The **trachea (windpipe)** is a tube that carries air from your throat down to your lungs. It's held open by C-shaped rings of cartilage, which you can feel at the front of your neck."],
  ["why does food go down the wrong pipe | what is the epiglottis | why do i choke on water sometimes", "A little flap called the **epiglottis** covers your windpipe when you swallow, so food goes down your food pipe instead. If you talk or laugh while swallowing, it might not close in time, and you **cough** to push the food or drink back out."],
  ["how do we talk | how does the voice box work | what is the larynx", "Your **larynx (voice box)** in your throat holds two **vocal cords**. When air from your lungs passes between them, they vibrate and make sound. Your tongue, lips and teeth shape that sound into words."],
  ["why can't humans breathe underwater | why can fish breathe underwater but we can't", "Our **lungs** are built to take oxygen from air, not water. Fish have **gills** that pull dissolved oxygen out of water. Water is too heavy and has too little oxygen for our lungs to use."],
  ["why can't you hold your breath forever | what happens when you hold your breath | why do i have to breathe after holding my breath", "As you hold your breath, **carbon dioxide builds up** in your blood. Your brainstem senses it and creates a powerful urge to breathe, so you can't hold it forever. It's your body's safety system."],
  ["why do i get out of breath when running | why do we breathe hard during exercise | why am i panting", "Your working muscles need extra oxygen and make extra carbon dioxide, so your brain makes you **breathe faster and deeper** to keep up. Regular exercise makes your heart and lungs more efficient, so you get less winded over time."],
  ["why do people snore | what causes snoring | what is snoring", "**Snoring** happens when the soft tissues in your throat **relax and vibrate** as air squeezes past during sleep. Colds, allergies and sleeping on your back can make it worse. Very loud snoring with pauses in breathing should be checked by a doctor."],
  ["why do we cough | what is a cough for | what causes coughing", "A **cough** is a reflex that **clears your airways** of mucus, dust, germs or food that went down the wrong way. Colds often cause coughs. See a doctor for a cough that lasts more than a few weeks, or for trouble breathing."],
  ["what is mucus | what is snot | why do we have snot", "**Mucus (snot)** is a sticky fluid that lines your nose and airways. It **traps dust, pollen and germs** before they reach your lungs. Your nose makes it all day; you just notice it more when you're sick."],
  ["why is snot green | why does snot change color | what does green mucus mean", "When you're fighting an infection, immune cells rush to your nose. They contain a greenish enzyme, which can turn snot **yellow or green**. The color alone doesn't tell you if it's a virus or bacteria. See a doctor if you're sick for more than about 10 days or getting worse."],
  ["why does my nose run when it's cold | why does cold weather make your nose run", "Your nose **warms and moistens** the air you breathe. In cold air it makes extra fluid to do this, and water vapor from your breath can condense inside your nose, so it drips."],
  ["why do we have nose hairs | what do nose hairs do", "**Nose hairs** act like a filter, catching dust, pollen and other particles before they get into your lungs. Together with mucus, they're your airway's first line of defense."],
  ["why is it hard to breathe on a mountain | why do people get altitude sickness | why is there less oxygen up high", "High up, the air is **thinner**, so each breath brings in less oxygen. Your body breathes faster to make up for it, and going up too quickly can cause **altitude sickness** (headache, tiredness, nausea). Climbers go up slowly so their bodies can adjust."],
  ["what are the steps of digestion | how does food get digested | what happens to food after you eat it | digestion steps", "Digestion step by step:\n1. **Mouth**: teeth chew, saliva starts breaking down starch\n2. **Esophagus**: muscles squeeze food down to the stomach\n3. **Stomach**: acid and enzymes churn food into a mushy liquid\n4. **Small intestine**: nutrients are broken down and absorbed into blood\n5. **Large intestine**: water is absorbed and waste forms\n6. Waste leaves the body as poop"],
  ["what does saliva do | why do we have spit | how much spit do we make", "**Saliva** moistens food so you can swallow it, contains enzymes that start breaking down starch, helps you taste, and protects your teeth. You make about **1 to 1.5 liters** a day!"],
  ["what is the esophagus | how does food get to your stomach | what is the food pipe", "The **esophagus** is a muscular tube from your throat to your stomach. Waves of muscle squeezes called **peristalsis** push food down, which is why you could even swallow while upside down."],
  ["what is stomach acid | why doesn't stomach acid burn the stomach | how strong is stomach acid", "Your stomach makes **hydrochloric acid**, strong enough to help break down food and kill many germs. A thick layer of **mucus** protects the stomach wall, and the lining replaces itself every few days."],
  ["what does the small intestine do | what are villi | where are nutrients absorbed", "The **small intestine** is where most nutrients get absorbed into your blood. It's about **6 meters (20 feet)** long and lined with millions of tiny finger-like bumps called **villi** that give it a huge surface area."],
  ["what does the large intestine do | what is the colon | what does the colon do", "The **large intestine (colon)** is about **1.5 meters (5 feet)** long. It absorbs water and salts from leftover food, is home to trillions of helpful bacteria, and forms waste into poop."],
  ["how long does it take to digest food | how long does food stay in your stomach | how long from eating to pooping", "Food usually stays in the stomach for **2 to 4 hours**. The whole trip through your digestive system typically takes about **1 to 3 days**, depending on what you ate and your body."],
  ["what does the gallbladder do | what is bile | where is bile made", "**Bile** is a greenish-yellow liquid made by your **liver** that helps break fats into tiny droplets so they can be digested. The **gallbladder** is a small pouch that stores bile and squeezes it into the small intestine when you eat."],
  ["what does the pancreas do | what is the pancreas | where is the pancreas", "The **pancreas** sits behind your stomach and has two big jobs: it makes **digestive enzymes** to break down food, and it makes hormones like **insulin** that control your blood sugar."],
  ["what is appendicitis | why does the appendix get inflamed | appendicitis symptoms", "**Appendicitis** is when the appendix, a small pouch on the large intestine, becomes blocked and inflamed. It usually causes belly pain that starts near the belly button and moves to the **lower right side**, often with fever or vomiting. It needs a doctor **right away**, and the usual fix is surgery to remove it."],
  ["why does my stomach growl | why does your tummy rumble | what makes stomach noises", "Stomach growls happen when your digestive muscles squeeze **gas and liquid** through your intestines. It's louder when your stomach is empty because there's nothing to muffle the sound. The noise even has a name: **borborygmi**!"],
  ["why do we burp | what causes burping | what is a burp", "A **burp** lets out extra **air or gas** from your stomach. You swallow air when you eat fast, talk while eating, or drink fizzy drinks. Eating slowly helps you burp less."],
  ["why do we fart | why do we pass gas | what causes gas", "Passing gas is totally normal! It comes from swallowed air and from **gut bacteria** breaking down food like beans and fiber, which releases gases. Most people pass gas around **10 to 20 times a day**."],
  ["why is poop brown | what gives poop its color | what is poop made of", "Poop is mostly **water, bacteria, fiber and old cells**. It's brown because of **bilirubin**, a substance from old red blood cells that gets changed by bacteria in your gut. If poop is ever black, red or very pale, tell an adult and see a doctor."],
  ["what is the gut microbiome | what is gut bacteria | are there bacteria in your stomach", "Your intestines are home to **trillions of bacteria**, called the gut microbiome. Most are helpful: they digest fiber, make some vitamins, train your immune system and keep harmful germs in check. Eating fiber-rich foods like vegetables, fruit, beans and whole grains feeds them."],
  ["why do we get hungry | what makes you feel hungry | what is the hunger hormone", "When your stomach is empty and energy runs low, it releases a hormone called **ghrelin** that tells your brain it's time to eat. After eating, other hormones like **leptin** help signal that you're full."],
  ["why do we feel full | how does your body know you're full | why should you eat slowly", "As your stomach stretches and nutrients reach your gut, hormones tell your brain **'that's enough'**. This signal takes about **20 minutes**, so eating slowly helps you notice when you're full."],
  ["what is heartburn | why does my chest burn after eating | what is acid reflux", "**Heartburn** has nothing to do with your heart. It's when **stomach acid splashes up** into your esophagus, causing a burning feeling in your chest. Big meals, spicy or fatty foods and lying down right after eating can trigger it. Frequent heartburn should be checked by a doctor."],
  ["what is constipation | why can't i poop | how to help constipation", "**Constipation** means poop is hard, dry or difficult to pass. Common helpers are **drinking more water, eating more fiber** (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, beans) and staying active. If it lasts a long time or hurts a lot, see a doctor."],
  ["why do we throw up | what makes you vomit | why do people get sick to their stomach", "**Vomiting** is your body's way of getting rid of something it thinks is harmful, like germs or spoiled food. It can also happen with motion sickness. Sip water in small amounts to stay hydrated, and see a doctor if it won't stop, there's blood, or you can't keep fluids down."],
  ["what is lactose intolerance | why does milk upset my stomach | what is lactose", "**Lactose** is the natural sugar in milk. People with **lactose intolerance** don't make enough of the enzyme lactase to digest it, so dairy can cause bloating, gas or tummy aches. It's different from a milk allergy. A doctor can help figure it out."],
  ["what is gluten | what is celiac disease | what does gluten free mean", "**Gluten** is a protein found in wheat, barley and rye. People with **celiac disease** have an immune reaction to gluten that damages their small intestine, so they must eat gluten-free. Most people digest gluten just fine."],
  ["food allergy vs intolerance | what is the difference between a food allergy and an intolerance", "A **food allergy** involves your **immune system** and can be serious, even life-threatening (like a peanut allergy). A **food intolerance** is a digestion problem that causes discomfort, like gas or a tummy ache, but isn't dangerous in the same way."],
  ["what is metabolism | what does metabolism mean | how does metabolism work", "**Metabolism** is all the chemical reactions in your body that turn food into energy and building blocks. Even resting, your body uses energy to breathe, pump blood, think and stay warm. Age, size, muscle and activity all affect it."],
  ["what are white blood cells | what do white blood cells do | types of white blood cells", "**White blood cells** are your body's defenders. Some, like **phagocytes**, gobble up germs; **B cells** make antibodies; and **T cells** destroy infected cells and coordinate the attack. You have billions patrolling your body."],
  ["what are antibodies | how do antibodies work | what do antibodies do", "**Antibodies** are Y-shaped proteins made by your immune system. Each one fits a specific germ like a key in a lock, **sticking to it** so it can't infect cells and flagging it for destruction."],
  ["what is an antigen | antigen vs antibody | what are antigens", "An **antigen** is a marker, usually on the surface of a germ, that your immune system recognizes as foreign. **Antibodies** are what your body makes to attach to those antigens."],
  ["what is innate and adaptive immunity | innate vs adaptive immune system | two parts of the immune system", "Your immune system has two teams:\n- **Innate immunity** reacts fast to any invader (skin, mucus, fever, inflammation, germ-eating cells)\n- **Adaptive immunity** is slower at first but learns to target a specific germ, makes antibodies and **remembers** it for next time"],
  ["why do we get fevers | why does your temperature go up when sick | is a fever good", "A **fever** is your immune system turning up your body's thermostat to make it harder for germs to multiply and to speed up your defenses. It's usually a sign your body is fighting. Rest, drink fluids and tell an adult; see a doctor for a very high fever, a fever lasting more than a few days, or a fever in a baby."],
  ["why do we get chills with a fever | why do i feel cold when i have a fever", "When a fever starts, your brain raises your body's target temperature. Until you reach it, you feel **cold and shivery** because your body is trying to heat up. When the fever breaks, you may feel hot and sweaty instead."],
  ["what is inflammation | why do cuts get red and swollen | why does an injury swell", "**Inflammation** is your body's repair response. Blood vessels widen to bring more blood and immune cells to the injury, making it **red, warm, swollen and sore**. It's a sign of healing. Tell an adult if redness spreads, it gets very painful, or there's pus and fever."],
  ["what is pus | why do wounds have pus | what is pus made of", "**Pus** is a thick yellowish fluid made of **dead white blood cells**, germs and tissue. It shows your body is fighting an infection. A wound with pus should be kept clean, and an adult should check it; see a doctor if it's spreading or you get a fever."],
  ["what is herd immunity | what is community immunity | how does herd immunity work", "**Herd immunity** is when enough people in a community are immune to a disease, through vaccination or past infection, that it can't spread easily. This helps protect people who can't be vaccinated, like newborn babies or people with certain illnesses."],
  ["what is in a vaccine | what are vaccines made of | types of vaccines", "Vaccines contain something that teaches your immune system to recognize a germ without making you sick, such as:\n- A **weakened** or **inactivated** (killed) germ\n- A harmless **piece** of the germ, like a protein\n- **Instructions (mRNA or a harmless viral vector)** that tell your cells to make a harmless piece of the germ"],
  ["what is an mrna vaccine | how do mrna vaccines work | does mrna change your dna", "An **mRNA vaccine** gives your cells temporary instructions to make a harmless protein from a germ. Your immune system learns to recognize it. The mRNA breaks down within days and **does not enter the nucleus or change your DNA**."],
  ["are vaccines safe | are vaccines tested | what are vaccine side effects", "Vaccines are **tested in large studies** before they're approved and are **monitored** afterward. Common side effects are mild and short-lived, like a sore arm, tiredness or a low fever. Serious reactions are rare. If you have questions about a specific vaccine, ask a doctor or nurse."],
  ["what is a booster shot | why do we need booster shots | why do some vaccines need more than one dose", "A **booster** is an extra vaccine dose that reminds your immune system about a germ. For some vaccines, protection fades over time or the germ changes, so a booster helps keep your immune memory strong. Doctors follow a schedule for which boosters you need."],
  ["how does immune memory work | what are memory cells | why don't you get some diseases twice", "After you fight off a germ (or get vaccinated), your body keeps **memory B and T cells** that recognize it. If the same germ shows up again, they launch a **faster, stronger** attack, often before you even feel sick. That's why you usually only get chickenpox once."],
  ["what is an autoimmune disease | what does autoimmune mean | what is an autoimmune condition", "An **autoimmune disease** happens when the immune system mistakenly attacks the body's own healthy cells. Examples include **type 1 diabetes**, where it attacks insulin-making cells. Doctors can help manage these conditions."],
  ["what is histamine | what causes allergy symptoms | why do allergies make you sneeze", "**Histamine** is a chemical your immune cells release when they think something is harmful. In allergies, it's released by mistake in response to harmless things like pollen, causing **sneezing, itching, watery eyes and a runny nose**."],
  ["do antibiotics work on viruses | why won't antibiotics help a cold | can antibiotics cure the flu", "**Antibiotics** are medicines that kill or stop **bacteria**. They **don't work on viruses**, so they can't cure a cold or the flu. Only a doctor can decide if you need antibiotics, and they should be taken exactly as prescribed."],
  ["what is antibiotic resistance | what are superbugs | why can antibiotics stop working", "**Antibiotic resistance** happens when bacteria evolve so that medicines no longer kill them. Overusing antibiotics speeds this up. That's why they should only be used when a doctor prescribes them, and taken the way the doctor says."],
  ["what is a pandemic | pandemic vs epidemic | what is an outbreak", "An **outbreak** is more cases of a disease than expected in one area. An **epidemic** is a larger spread across a region or country. A **pandemic** is when a disease spreads across **many countries and continents**."],
  ["what does contagious mean | how long are you contagious | what is an incubation period", "**Contagious** means an illness can spread from person to person. The **incubation period** is the time between catching a germ and feeling sick, and with some illnesses you can spread it even before symptoms appear. A doctor can tell you when it's safe to go back to school."],
  ["how do i keep my immune system healthy | how to boost your immune system | how to stay healthy and not get sick", "Healthy habits help your immune system do its job:\n- Sleep enough\n- Eat lots of fruits and vegetables\n- Stay active\n- Wash your hands often\n- Stay up to date on vaccines recommended by your doctor\n- Manage stress"],
  ["what are tonsils | what do tonsils do | why do people get their tonsils out", "**Tonsils** are two lumps of immune tissue at the back of your throat that help catch germs coming in through your mouth and nose. Sometimes they get infected often or are too big, and a doctor may recommend removing them."],
  ["what is the thymus | what does the thymus do | where is the thymus", "The **thymus** is a small organ behind your breastbone where **T cells** (a type of white blood cell) are trained to fight germs. It's most active in childhood and slowly shrinks after puberty."],
  ["what is the endocrine system | what does the endocrine system do | what are glands", "The **endocrine system** is a group of **glands** that make **hormones**, chemical messengers carried in your blood. Hormones control growth, energy use, sleep, mood, puberty and much more."],
  ["what are hormones | what do hormones do | how do hormones work", "**Hormones** are chemical messengers released by glands into your blood. They travel to specific organs and tell them what to do, like **insulin** telling cells to take in sugar or **growth hormone** helping you grow."],
  ["what is the pituitary gland | what is the master gland | what does the pituitary gland do", "The **pituitary gland** is a pea-sized gland under your brain, often called the **master gland**. It releases hormones that control growth and tell other glands (like the thyroid and adrenal glands) what to do."],
  ["what does the thyroid do | what is the thyroid gland | where is the thyroid", "The **thyroid** is a butterfly-shaped gland in the front of your neck. Its hormones control your **metabolism**, how fast your body uses energy, and affect growth, heart rate and body temperature. It needs **iodine** from food to work."],
  ["what are the adrenal glands | what do adrenal glands do | where are the adrenal glands", "The **adrenal glands** sit on top of your kidneys. They make **adrenaline** for quick fight-or-flight reactions, **cortisol** to help handle stress and control blood sugar, and other hormones that balance salt and water."],
  ["what is adrenaline | what does adrenaline do | what is an adrenaline rush", "**Adrenaline** is a hormone released when you're excited, scared or in danger. It speeds up your heart, sends extra blood to your muscles and sharpens your focus, getting you ready to act fast. That's the 'rush' you feel on a roller coaster."],
  ["what is fight or flight | what is the fight or flight response | why do we freeze when scared", "**Fight-or-flight** is your body's automatic reaction to danger. Your heart races, breathing speeds up, muscles tense and digestion slows so you can **fight, run, or freeze**. It's helpful in real danger, and slow breathing can calm it when the danger isn't real."],
  ["what is cortisol | what is the stress hormone | what does cortisol do", "**Cortisol** is often called the **stress hormone**. It helps your body handle stress by raising blood sugar for energy, and it also follows a daily rhythm, highest in the morning to help you wake up. Too much for too long isn't good, which is why rest and relaxation matter."],
  ["what is insulin | what does insulin do | how does the body control blood sugar", "**Insulin** is a hormone from the pancreas that acts like a key, letting **sugar (glucose)** move from your blood into your cells for energy. Another hormone, **glucagon**, releases stored sugar when levels drop. Together they keep blood sugar balanced."],
  ["what is diabetes | type 1 vs type 2 diabetes | what causes diabetes", "**Diabetes** is when the body can't control blood sugar properly.\n- **Type 1**: the body stops making insulin, so people need insulin from a doctor's treatment plan\n- **Type 2**: the body doesn't use insulin well; healthy eating, exercise and doctor-guided treatment help manage it"],
  ["what is blood sugar | what is glucose | why does blood sugar matter", "**Blood sugar (glucose)** is the main fuel for your cells, especially your brain. It comes from carbohydrates you eat. Your body keeps it in a healthy range; too low can make you shaky and tired, and too high over a long time can damage organs."],
  ["what is growth hormone | what makes you grow | when does growth hormone get released", "**Growth hormone** comes from your pituitary gland and helps bones and muscles grow. Much of it is released during **deep sleep**, which is one reason sleep is so important for growing kids."],
  ["what is melatonin | what makes you sleepy at night | what does the pineal gland do", "**Melatonin** is a hormone made by the **pineal gland** in your brain when it gets dark. It tells your body it's time to sleep. Bright screens at night can delay it, which is why dimming lights before bed helps."],
  ["what are testosterone and estrogen | what are sex hormones | what hormones cause puberty", "**Testosterone** and **estrogen** are hormones that guide the body changes of puberty. Everyone has both, in different amounts. They help with growth, bone strength, voice changes, body hair and the development of reproductive organs."],
  ["what is the urinary system | how does the body make pee | what are the parts of the urinary system", "The **urinary system** removes waste and extra water from your blood. The **kidneys** filter blood and make urine, two tubes called **ureters** carry it to the **bladder** for storage, and it leaves through the **urethra** when you pee."],
  ["how do kidneys filter blood | what are nephrons | how much blood do the kidneys filter", "Each kidney has about **1 million tiny filters called nephrons**. Together, your kidneys filter about **180 liters of fluid** from your blood every day, keeping what you need and sending waste and extra water out as urine (about 1 to 2 liters a day)."],
  ["why is pee yellow | what gives urine its color | what color should pee be", "Pee gets its yellow color from **urochrome**, a pigment from broken-down old red blood cells. **Pale yellow** usually means you're well hydrated; dark yellow often means you need more water. Red, brown or cloudy pee with pain should be checked by a doctor."],
  ["how much can the bladder hold | how big is your bladder | why do i have to pee so often", "An adult bladder can comfortably hold about **400 to 600 ml** (roughly 2 cups); kids' bladders hold less. When it fills, nerves tell your brain it's time to go. Drinking lots, cold weather and caffeine can make you pee more often."],
  ["can you live with one kidney | do you need both kidneys | how many kidneys do you need", "Yes, people can live healthy lives with **just one kidney**. The remaining kidney grows a bit and takes over the work. That's why some people can donate a kidney to someone who needs it."],
  ["what are kidney stones | what causes kidney stones | how to prevent kidney stones", "**Kidney stones** are hard little crystals that form from minerals in urine. They can be very painful when they pass. **Drinking plenty of water** is one of the best ways to help prevent them."],
  ["what is the integumentary system | what does skin do | why is skin important", "Your skin, hair and nails form the **integumentary system**. Skin keeps germs out, holds water in, controls body temperature through sweat, makes **vitamin D** from sunlight, and senses touch, heat, cold and pain."],
  ["why do people have different skin colors | what gives skin its color | what is melanin", "Skin color comes mostly from **melanin**, a pigment made by skin cells called melanocytes. People whose ancestors lived where sunlight is strong tend to have more melanin, which helps protect against UV rays. Everyone has about the same number of melanocytes; they just make different amounts."],
  ["what are freckles | why do people get freckles | why do freckles get darker in summer", "**Freckles** are small spots where skin cells make extra **melanin**. They're often inherited and become darker with sun exposure, which is why they pop out more in summer. They're harmless."],
  ["why do we get sunburned | what is a sunburn | what causes sunburn", "A **sunburn** is skin damage from the sun's **UV rays**, making skin red, hot and sore. Protect yourself with **sunscreen** (SPF 30 or more), hats, sunglasses and shade, especially between late morning and mid-afternoon. Reapply sunscreen after swimming or sweating."],
  ["what does spf mean | how does sunscreen work | what spf should i use", "**SPF** stands for **Sun Protection Factor**, and it measures how well sunscreen blocks UVB rays that cause sunburn. Experts usually suggest **SPF 30 or higher**, **broad spectrum**, applied generously and reapplied about every 2 hours."],
  ["why do people tan | what is a suntan | is tanning healthy", "A **tan** is your skin making more **melanin** to protect itself after UV damage. That means a tan is actually a sign of damage, not health. Using sunscreen and shade is the safest way to enjoy the sun."],
  ["what are moles | why do we have moles | when should you check a mole", "**Moles** are small clusters of pigment cells and are usually harmless. Tell an adult and see a doctor if a mole **changes size, shape or color**, has uneven edges, bleeds or itches."],
  ["why do we get pimples | what causes acne | how to get rid of pimples", "**Pimples** form when **pores get clogged** with oil and dead skin, and bacteria cause swelling. They're very common during puberty because hormones make skin oilier. Washing your face gently twice a day and not squeezing pimples helps. A doctor or dermatologist can help with bad acne."],
  ["what are pores | why do we have pores | what are skin pores for", "**Pores** are tiny openings in your skin. Some release **sweat** to cool you down, and others release **oil (sebum)** from hair follicles to keep skin and hair soft."],
  ["how often does skin replace itself | how many skin cells do we lose | is dust made of dead skin", "You shed about **30,000 to 40,000 dead skin cells every minute**, and your outer skin layer renews itself roughly **every month**. Dead skin is one part of household dust, along with dirt, fibers, pollen and more."],
  ["why do fingers get wrinkly in water | why do fingers prune in the bath | why do we get pruney fingers", "After a long soak, your **nervous system** shrinks blood vessels under the skin of your fingers and toes, making it pucker into wrinkles. Scientists think this helps you **grip wet things** better, like tire treads. They smooth out once you're dry."],
  ["what is a scar | why do scars form | do scars go away", "A **scar** forms when the deeper layer of skin is damaged and your body patches it with **collagen fibers**, which look different from normal skin. Scars often fade over months or years but may not disappear completely. Keeping wounds clean and protected from the sun helps."],
  ["what is a blister | why do we get blisters | should you pop a blister", "A **blister** is a bubble of fluid that forms to cushion and protect skin that's been rubbed or burned. It's best **not to pop it**, since the skin covering protects against germs. Cover it with a bandage and let it heal."],
  ["why do we get goosebumps | what causes goosebumps | what are goosebumps", "**Goosebumps** happen when tiny muscles at the base of hair follicles contract and pull hairs upright. It happens when you're cold or have strong emotions. In furry animals this fluffs up fur for warmth or to look bigger; in humans it's a leftover reflex."],
  ["why does skin itch | what causes itching | why shouldn't you scratch", "**Itching** can come from dry skin, bug bites, allergies, rashes or healing wounds. Scratching feels good briefly but can break the skin and let germs in. Cool cloths and moisturizer often help. See a doctor for itching that's severe or won't go away."],
  ["why do mosquito bites itch | what happens when a mosquito bites you", "When a mosquito bites, it injects a little **saliva** to keep your blood flowing. Your immune system reacts to it by releasing **histamine**, which causes the itchy red bump. Try not to scratch; a cold cloth can help."],
  ["what is hair made of | is hair alive | why do we have hair", "Hair is made of a tough protein called **keratin**. The part you see is **not alive**, which is why haircuts don't hurt; only the root in the follicle is living. Hair helps keep you warm, protects your skin from the sun and helps you sense touch."],
  ["why does hair turn gray | why do people get gray hair | why does hair turn white", "Hair gets its color from **melanin** made by cells in the hair follicle. As we age, these cells gradually **make less pigment**, so new hair grows in **gray or white**. When it happens depends mostly on your genes."],
  ["how fast does hair grow | how much does hair grow in a month | how many hairs are on your head", "Scalp hair grows about **1 cm (half an inch) a month**, around 15 cm (6 inches) a year. Most people have about **100,000 hairs** on their head and lose **50 to 100 a day**, which is normal as new ones replace them."],
  ["why is some hair curly and some straight | what makes hair curly | why do people have different hair", "The **shape of your hair follicle** decides the texture: round follicles grow straight hair, while oval or flat ones grow wavy or curly hair. Your genes mostly determine which you have."],
  ["what are nails made of | why do we have fingernails | how fast do nails grow", "Nails are made of **keratin**, the same protein as hair. They protect your fingertips and help you pick up small things. Fingernails grow about **3 mm a month**, and toenails grow more slowly."],
  ["what are calluses | why does skin get hard | why do i have hard skin on my hands", "**Calluses** are thick, tough patches of skin that form where there's lots of rubbing or pressure, like on guitar players' fingertips or the bottoms of feet. They're the skin's way of protecting itself."],
  ["what is eczema | why is my skin dry and itchy | what causes eczema", "**Eczema** is a common condition that makes skin **dry, red and itchy**. It often runs in families and can flare up with soaps, weather or allergies. Gentle soap and moisturizer help, and a doctor can suggest a treatment plan."],
  ["what are warts | what causes warts | are warts contagious", "**Warts** are small rough bumps caused by a common **virus** that infects the top layer of skin. They can spread by touch, so avoid picking them. Many go away on their own; a doctor can help remove stubborn ones."],
  ["why do we have eyebrows | what are eyebrows for | why do we have eyelashes", "**Eyebrows** help keep sweat and rain from dripping into your eyes, and they help show emotions. **Eyelashes** catch dust and trigger a blink when something gets too close to your eyes."],
  ["why does sweat smell | what causes body odor | why do armpits smell", "Fresh sweat barely smells. Body odor happens when **bacteria on your skin** break down sweat, especially from glands in your armpits that become active during puberty. Showering regularly, clean clothes and deodorant help."],
  ["why do we shiver | what is shivering | why do we shake when cold", "**Shivering** is your muscles quickly tightening and relaxing over and over to **make heat**. Your brain starts it automatically when your body temperature drops, to warm you back up."],
  ["why do teeth chatter when cold | why does your jaw shake when you're cold", "Chattering teeth are just **shivering of your jaw muscles**. They rapidly contract to make heat, which makes your teeth clack together."],
  ["why do we blush | what makes your face turn red | why do cheeks get red when embarrassed", "When you feel embarrassed, **adrenaline** makes blood vessels in your face widen, so more blood flows to your cheeks and they turn red. It's an automatic reaction you can't easily control, and everyone does it."],
  ["do we have more than five senses | how many senses do humans have | what is proprioception", "Yes! Besides the classic five, you also sense **balance**, **temperature**, **pain**, **hunger and thirst**, and **proprioception**, your sense of where your body parts are, which lets you touch your nose with your eyes closed."],
  ["what are the parts of the eye | what is the retina | what is the cornea", "Main parts of the eye:\n- **Cornea**: clear front window that bends light\n- **Iris**: colored part that controls the pupil\n- **Pupil**: the dark opening that lets light in\n- **Lens**: focuses light\n- **Retina**: light-sensing layer at the back\n- **Optic nerve**: carries signals to the brain"],
  ["what are rods and cones | what are cone cells | how does the eye see colors", "Your retina has two kinds of light sensors. **Rods** (about 120 million) work in dim light but only see in grayscale. **Cones** (about 6 million) need brighter light and detect **red, green and blue**, which your brain mixes to see roughly a million colors."],
  ["why do pupils get bigger and smaller | why do pupils change size | why are my pupils big", "Your **pupils** get **smaller in bright light** to protect your eye and **bigger in dim light** to let more light in. They can also widen when you're excited or scared."],
  ["what gives eyes their color | why do people have different eye colors | why are some eyes blue", "Eye color depends on the amount of **melanin** in the **iris**. Brown eyes have a lot. Blue eyes have very little, and their blue color comes from the way light **scatters** in the iris, similar to why the sky looks blue."],
  ["what is color blindness | why are some people color blind | what do color blind people see", "**Color blindness** means some cone cells in the eye don't work the usual way, making certain colors (often **red and green**) hard to tell apart. It's usually inherited and is more common in boys, affecting about **1 in 12 males** and **1 in 200 females**."],
  ["what is nearsighted and farsighted | nearsighted vs farsighted | what does nearsighted mean", "**Nearsighted** people see close things clearly but far things look blurry. **Farsighted** people see far things better than close things. Both happen because the eye focuses light slightly in front of or behind the retina, and **glasses or contacts** fix it."],
  ["why do people need glasses | how do glasses work | what does an eye exam check", "**Glasses** have lenses that bend light so it focuses exactly on your retina, making things sharp. An **eye exam** checks how clearly you see and the health of your eyes. If the board at school looks blurry or you get headaches from reading, tell an adult."],
  ["what does 20/20 vision mean | what is perfect vision | what does 20 20 mean", "**20/20 vision** means you can see at 20 feet what a typical person can see at 20 feet: normal sharpness. 20/40 means you need to be at 20 feet to see what most people can see from 40 feet away."],
  ["why do we blink | how often do we blink | what is blinking for", "Blinking **spreads tears** over your eyes to keep them moist and clean and protects them from dust. People blink about **15 to 20 times a minute**, but much less when staring at screens, which can make eyes feel dry."],
  ["why do we cry | what are tears made of | why do we have tears", "**Tears** are mostly water with salt, oils and germ-fighting proteins. Your eyes make tears all the time to stay moist, extra tears to wash away irritants, and **emotional tears** when you feel strong feelings. Crying can actually help you feel calmer afterward."],
  ["what is the blind spot | do we have a blind spot in our eye | why don't we notice our blind spot", "Each eye has a **blind spot** where the optic nerve leaves the retina; there are no light sensors there. You don't notice it because your **brain fills in the gap** and your two eyes cover for each other."],
  ["why do my eyes get red | what causes red eyes | why are my eyes bloodshot", "Eyes look red when tiny blood vessels on the surface **widen**, often from tiredness, rubbing, dryness, allergies, swimming pools or an infection. If your eye is red with pain, sticky discharge or vision changes, see a doctor."],
  ["what are eye floaters | why do i see squiggly lines | what are the specks in my vision", "**Floaters** are tiny clumps in the clear gel inside your eye that cast shadows on your retina, looking like drifting specks or squiggles. They're usually harmless. A **sudden shower of floaters or flashes of light** needs a doctor right away."],
  ["why do i see stars when i rub my eyes | why do you see colors when you press your eyes", "Pressing or rubbing your eyes puts **pressure on the retina**, which triggers its cells as if they were seeing light. Your brain interprets that as sparkles or colors, called **phosphenes**. Rubbing your eyes a lot isn't great for them, so go gently."],
  ["what are the parts of the ear | what is the eardrum | what is the cochlea", "The ear has 3 parts:\n- **Outer ear**: collects sound down the ear canal to the **eardrum**\n- **Middle ear**: three tiny bones (hammer, anvil, stirrup) pass on the vibrations\n- **Inner ear**: the snail-shaped **cochlea** turns vibrations into nerve signals, and nearby canals help with balance"],
  ["why do my ears pop | what makes ears pop on a plane | how to pop your ears", "Your ears **pop** when air pressure changes, like on a plane or mountain road. A small tube called the **Eustachian tube** opens to balance pressure inside your middle ear. **Swallowing, yawning or chewing gum** helps open it."],
  ["what is earwax | why do we have earwax | should i use cotton swabs in my ears", "**Earwax** cleans, moisturizes and protects your ear canal by trapping dust and germs. It usually moves out on its own. **Don't put cotton swabs** deep in your ear, since that can push wax in or hurt your eardrum. If your ear feels blocked, see a doctor."],
  ["how do we keep our balance | what controls balance | how does the inner ear help balance", "Your **inner ear** has fluid-filled loops (semicircular canals) that sense head movement. Your brain combines that with what your **eyes** see and what your **muscles and joints** feel to keep you balanced."],
  ["what causes motion sickness | why do people get motion sickness | why do i feel sick reading in the car", "**Motion sickness** happens when your **inner ear feels movement** but your eyes (say, reading a book) tell your brain you're still. The mixed signals can make you feel queasy. Looking out the window at the horizon, fresh air and not reading in the car often help."],
  ["how loud is too loud | can headphones damage hearing | how to protect your hearing", "Long exposure to sounds over about **85 decibels** (like heavy traffic) can damage hearing permanently. Try the **60/60 rule**: headphones at no more than 60% volume for about 60 minutes at a time, and wear earplugs at loud events. If you have to shout to be heard, it's too loud."],
  ["why do we have two ears | why do we have two eyes | why do we have two of some organs", "Two ears help your brain figure out **where sounds come from**, by comparing tiny differences in timing and loudness. Two eyes give **depth perception**, letting you judge distances in 3D."],
  ["why does my voice sound weird on recordings | why do i hate hearing my own voice", "When you talk, you hear your voice through the air **and** through vibrations in your **skull bones**, which add deeper tones. A recording only captures the air version, so it sounds higher and unfamiliar to you, but it's how everyone else hears you."],
  ["is the tongue taste map real | does the tongue have taste zones | different parts of tongue taste different things", "The idea that each area of the tongue tastes only one flavor is a **myth**. Taste buds all over your tongue can detect **all five basic tastes**: sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami."],
  ["how many taste buds do we have | what are taste buds | do taste buds grow back", "You have roughly **2,000 to 10,000 taste buds**, mostly on your tongue. They're tucked inside the little bumps (papillae). Taste cells are replaced about **every 1 to 2 weeks**, so a burned tongue usually recovers."],
  ["why does food taste bland when you have a cold | why can't i taste food when sick | how does smell affect taste", "Much of what we call flavor actually comes from **smell**. When a cold blocks your nose, aromas can't reach your smell sensors, so food tastes bland even though your tongue still works."],
  ["how many smells can humans detect | how good is the human sense of smell | is our sense of smell weak", "Humans can tell apart a huge number of smells, at least **thousands and possibly far more**. Our sense of smell is better than people often think, and it's strongly linked to **memory and emotion**."],
  ["how does touch work | what are touch receptors | which body parts are most sensitive", "Your skin has many kinds of **receptors** that sense light touch, pressure, vibration, heat, cold and pain. Your **fingertips, lips and tongue** are packed with them, which is why they're extra sensitive."],
  ["can you yawn in your sleep | do people yawn while sleeping | why do we yawn before bed", "Scientists still debate exactly why, but yawning may help **cool the brain** and boost alertness when you're sleepy or bored. People rarely yawn while fully asleep, but often do right before bed and right after waking up."],
  ["why do my eyes water when i yawn", "When you yawn, your face muscles squeeze around your eyes and press on your **tear glands**, pushing out a few tears. Totally normal!"],
  ["why do we sneeze | what makes you sneeze | why do we close our eyes when we sneeze", "A **sneeze** is a reflex that blasts irritants like dust, pollen or germs out of your nose. Your eyes close automatically as part of the reflex. Always sneeze into a **tissue or your elbow** to avoid spreading germs."],
  ["why do i sneeze when i look at the sun | what is the photic sneeze reflex | sun sneezing", "Some people sneeze when they suddenly step into bright light. It's called the **photic sneeze reflex**, and it's inherited, affecting roughly 1 in 4 people. Scientists think the nerve signals for light and sneezing get a little crossed."],
  ["can you sneeze with your eyes open | will your eyes pop out if you sneeze with them open", "Closing your eyes is an automatic part of the sneeze reflex, and it's hard to stop. But **your eyes won't pop out** if they stay open: that's a myth. Eyes are held in place by muscles, not your eyelids."],
  ["what is brain freeze | why do i get brain freeze | why does ice cream give you a headache", "**Brain freeze** happens when something very cold touches the roof of your mouth. Blood vessels there quickly narrow and then widen, and nearby nerves send a **sharp pain signal** your brain feels in your forehead. Pressing your **tongue against the roof of your mouth** helps it pass."],
  ["what are the stages of sleep | what is rem sleep | what happens during deep sleep", "Sleep happens in cycles of about **90 minutes**:\n- **Light sleep**: you drift off and can wake easily\n- **Deep sleep**: your body repairs itself and releases growth hormone\n- **REM sleep**: your brain is very active, your eyes dart around and most vivid dreams happen"],
  ["what is a circadian rhythm | what is your body clock | why do i get sleepy at the same time every day", "Your **circadian rhythm** is your body's natural 24-hour clock. It's set mainly by **light**, which tells your brain when to feel alert and when to release melatonin to feel sleepy. A regular bedtime and morning sunlight keep it on track."],
  ["what happens if you don't get enough sleep | what does lack of sleep do | is it bad to stay up all night", "Not getting enough sleep makes it harder to **focus, remember things and control emotions**. It can make you clumsy, grumpy and more likely to get sick. Your body and brain do important repair work while you sleep."],
  ["why do we forget our dreams | why can't i remember my dreams | how to remember dreams", "Your brain doesn't store memories strongly during sleep, so dreams fade within minutes of waking unless you think about them right away. Keeping a notebook by your bed and writing dreams down immediately helps."],
  ["why do people sleepwalk | what is sleepwalking | why do people talk in their sleep", "**Sleepwalking** and **sleep talking** happen when part of the brain wakes up while the rest stays in deep sleep. They're fairly common in kids and usually outgrown. Being very tired can make it more likely. A safe bedroom helps; frequent episodes are worth mentioning to a doctor."],
  ["why do we stretch when we wake up | why does stretching feel good in the morning", "After lying still, your muscles and joints get stiff. **Stretching** gets blood flowing, loosens muscles and helps wake up your body, which is why it feels so good. Even animals like cats and dogs do it."],
  ["why do i get tired after eating | why do we get sleepy after lunch | what is a food coma", "After a big meal, your body sends energy to **digestion**, and blood sugar changes can make you drowsy. There's also a natural **afternoon dip** in your body clock. Balanced meals that aren't huge can help."],
  ["why do i get butterflies in my stomach | what causes butterflies when nervous", "**Butterflies** come from the fight-or-flight response. When you're nervous or excited, **adrenaline** pulls blood away from your stomach toward your muscles, and your gut, which has lots of nerves, feels fluttery."],
  ["why do we laugh | what is laughing for | why is laughter contagious", "**Laughter** helps people **bond** and shows we're feeling safe and happy. Our brains are wired to copy others, so hearing laughter makes us more likely to laugh too. Laughing can also reduce stress."],
  ["why does my nose run when i cry", "Tears drain from the inner corners of your eyes through a little tube into your **nose**. When you cry a lot, extra tears flow into your nose, so it runs."],
  ["why do we get thirsty | what makes you feel thirsty | how does your body know it needs water", "When your body is low on water, your blood gets slightly more concentrated. Your **hypothalamus** senses this and makes you feel **thirsty**, and your kidneys hold onto water. Don't wait until you're very thirsty: sip water throughout the day."],
  ["why do we get headaches | what causes headaches | how to get rid of a headache", "Common causes of headaches include **not drinking enough water, skipping meals, poor sleep, stress, eye strain** from screens and illness. Resting, drinking water and a calm, dark room often help. Tell an adult about headaches that are severe, frequent, or come with a fever, stiff neck or after a head injury."],
  ["what is a migraine | migraine vs headache | what does a migraine feel like", "A **migraine** is a strong, often throbbing headache that can come with **sensitivity to light and sound**, nausea, or visual changes called an aura. Migraines often run in families. A doctor can help figure out triggers and a plan."],
  ["why do we get dark circles under our eyes | what causes eye bags", "**Dark circles** can come from tiredness, allergies, genetics, or simply thin skin under the eyes that lets blood vessels show through. Good sleep and drinking water may help, but some people just naturally have them."],
  ["what are growing pains | why do my legs hurt at night | are growing pains real", "**Growing pains** are aching or throbbing in the legs, usually at night, common in kids around ages 3 to 12. They're probably linked to active days rather than growth itself, and gentle stretching or massage helps. See a doctor if there's swelling, limping, fever or pain during the day."],
  ["why are most people right handed | what makes someone left handed | what is ambidextrous", "About **90% of people are right-handed** and about 10% left-handed. Handedness is shaped by genes and how the brain develops before birth. **Ambidextrous** people can use both hands equally well, which is rare."],
  ["why do we have a belly button | what is a belly button | what is the belly button for", "Your **belly button** is where your **umbilical cord** was attached before you were born. The cord brought you food and oxygen from your mother. After birth it's no longer needed, so it's clamped and falls off, leaving a little scar."],
  ["what is the uvula | what is the dangly thing in the back of my throat", "The **uvula** is the little teardrop of tissue hanging at the back of your throat. It helps **close off your nose** when you swallow so food and drink don't go up there, and it helps with some speech sounds."],
  ["what percentage of the body is water | how much of the body is water | why is the body mostly water", "Adults are roughly **60% water**, and babies even more. Water is in your blood, cells and tissues, helping carry nutrients, control temperature, cushion joints and remove waste."],
  ["how many times does the heart beat in a lifetime | how many heartbeats in a lifetime", "Over an average lifetime, the human heart beats about **2.5 to 3 billion times**, without ever taking a break."],
  ["how long can you live without water | how long can you live without food", "People can usually survive only about **3 days without water** but **several weeks without food**. That shows how important staying hydrated is!"],
  ["what are identical twins | identical vs fraternal twins | how do twins happen", "**Identical twins** come from one fertilized egg that splits in two, so they share almost the same DNA. **Fraternal twins** come from two separate eggs, so they're as alike as any siblings and can be different sexes."],
  ["why do we look like our parents | why do i look like my mom or dad | how are traits inherited", "You get **half your DNA from each parent**, so you inherit a mix of their genes for traits like eye color, hair, height and face shape. That's why you might have your mom's smile and your dad's eyes."],
  ["what is the placebo effect | what is a placebo | can believing make you feel better", "A **placebo** is a fake treatment, like a sugar pill. The **placebo effect** is when people feel better simply because they expect to. It shows how strongly the brain can affect how we feel, and it's why scientists test medicines carefully against placebos."],
  ["what is organ donation | what is an organ transplant | how do organ transplants work", "An **organ transplant** is surgery that replaces a failing organ with a healthy one from a **donor**. Some organs, like a kidney or part of a liver, can come from a living donor; others come from people who agreed to donate after they die. It saves many lives."],
  ["what is blood donation | why do people donate blood | who can donate blood", "**Donating blood** means giving a small amount of your blood so it can help people in surgery, accidents or with illnesses. Your body replaces it within weeks. Rules on age and weight vary by country, usually starting around **16 to 18 years old**."],
  ["what are nutrients | what nutrients does the body need | what are the six nutrients", "**Nutrients** are substances in food your body needs to grow, get energy and stay healthy. The six main types are **carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals and water**. Fiber is also important for digestion."],
  ["what are macronutrients | what are micronutrients | macronutrients vs micronutrients", "**Macronutrients** are the ones you need in large amounts and that give you energy: **carbohydrates, protein and fat**. **Micronutrients** are **vitamins and minerals**, needed in small amounts but still essential for your body to work."],
  ["what does protein do | why do we need protein | what is protein for", "**Protein** is your body's building material. It builds and repairs **muscles, skin, hair, organs and blood**, and makes enzymes, hormones and antibodies. It's made of smaller pieces called **amino acids**."],
  ["what are amino acids | what are essential amino acids | what is protein made of", "**Amino acids** are the building blocks of protein. Your body uses about 20 of them, and **9 are essential**, meaning you must get them from food. Animal foods, soy and quinoa contain all 9; mixing plant foods like beans and grains also covers them."],
  ["what are carbohydrates | what do carbs do | why do we need carbs", "**Carbohydrates** are your body's main **energy source**, especially for your brain and muscles. They're found in grains, fruits, vegetables, beans and milk. Your body breaks them down into glucose for fuel."],
  ["simple vs complex carbs | what are complex carbohydrates | what are simple sugars", "**Simple carbs** (sugars) digest quickly and give fast energy, like candy, juice and also fruit. **Complex carbs** (starches and fiber) digest slowly for steadier energy, like whole grains, beans, oats and potatoes. Most of your carbs should come from whole foods."],
  ["are carbs bad for you | should i avoid carbs | do carbs make you unhealthy", "**Carbs aren't bad**: they're your body's main fuel. The best choices are **whole grains, fruits, vegetables and beans**, which come with fiber and vitamins. It's mostly refined carbs and added sugars, like candy and soda, that are worth limiting."],
  ["what does fat do in the body | why do we need fat | is fat bad for you", "Your body **needs some fat**! It stores energy, keeps you warm, protects organs, builds cell walls and helps absorb **vitamins A, D, E and K**. The healthiest fats come from foods like nuts, seeds, avocados, olive oil and fish."],
  ["saturated vs unsaturated fat | what is unsaturated fat | what are healthy fats | good fats vs bad fats", "**Unsaturated fats** (olive oil, nuts, seeds, avocado, fish) are the healthier kind and are usually liquid at room temperature. **Saturated fats** (butter, fatty meat, cheese) are fine in smaller amounts. **Trans fats**, found in some processed foods, are the least healthy and are best avoided."],
  ["what are trans fats | why are trans fats bad | what foods have trans fat", "**Trans fats** are mostly made when liquid oils are processed into solid fats (partially hydrogenated oils). They raise 'bad' cholesterol and lower 'good' cholesterol, so many countries have limited them. Check labels for **partially hydrogenated oil**."],
  ["what are omega 3s | what is omega 3 good for | foods with omega 3", "**Omega-3s** are healthy fats important for your **brain, eyes and heart**. Good sources include **salmon, sardines and other oily fish**, walnuts, chia seeds and flaxseeds."],
  ["what is fiber | why is fiber important | what foods have fiber", "**Fiber** is the part of plant foods your body can't digest. It keeps your **digestion regular**, helps you feel full, feeds good gut bacteria and supports heart health. Find it in **beans, whole grains, fruits, vegetables, nuts and seeds**."],
  ["soluble vs insoluble fiber | what is soluble fiber | what is insoluble fiber", "**Soluble fiber** dissolves in water to form a gel that helps control blood sugar and cholesterol (oats, beans, apples). **Insoluble fiber** adds bulk and helps food move through your gut (whole wheat, vegetables, skins of fruit). Both are good for you."],
  ["what is a calorie | what are calories | why do we count calories", "A **calorie** is a unit of **energy** from food. Your body uses calories to do everything, from breathing to running. Carbs and protein have about 4 calories per gram, and fat has about 9. Growing kids need plenty of energy from healthy food, not strict calorie counting."],
  ["what does vitamin a do | foods with vitamin a | why is vitamin a important", "**Vitamin A** helps you **see in dim light**, keeps skin healthy and supports your immune system. Find it in **carrots, sweet potatoes, spinach, mangoes, eggs and milk**."],
  ["do carrots help you see in the dark | will carrots improve my eyesight | are carrots good for your eyes", "Carrots have **vitamin A**, which your eyes need to stay healthy, but eating lots of them **won't give you super night vision**. That idea was partly spread as a cover story during World War II! They're still a great healthy snack."],
  ["what do b vitamins do | what is vitamin b | foods with b vitamins", "The **B vitamins** (like B1, B2, B3, B6, B12 and folate) help your body **turn food into energy**, make red blood cells and keep nerves healthy. They're found in whole grains, meat, eggs, dairy, beans and leafy greens."],
  ["what does vitamin b12 do | where do you get b12 | vitamin b12 for vegans", "**Vitamin B12** helps make **red blood cells** and keeps your **nerves** healthy. It's found naturally in animal foods like meat, fish, eggs and dairy, so people who eat a vegan diet usually get it from **fortified foods or supplements**, with advice from a doctor."],
  ["what is folate | what does folic acid do | foods with folate", "**Folate** (vitamin B9, called folic acid when added to foods) helps your body make new cells and DNA. It's found in **leafy greens, beans, lentils, oranges** and fortified bread and cereals."],
  ["what does vitamin c do | foods with vitamin c | why do we need vitamin c", "**Vitamin C** helps heal wounds, keeps gums and skin healthy by making **collagen**, supports your immune system and helps you absorb iron. Great sources include **oranges, strawberries, kiwis, bell peppers, broccoli and tomatoes**."],
  ["what is scurvy | why did sailors get scurvy | what happens without vitamin c", "**Scurvy** is a disease caused by not getting enough **vitamin C**, causing bleeding gums, tiredness and slow-healing wounds. Long ago, sailors on long voyages without fresh fruit got it, until they learned that **citrus fruits** like lemons and limes prevented it."],
  ["what does vitamin d do | how do you get vitamin d | foods with vitamin d", "**Vitamin D** helps your body absorb **calcium** for strong bones and teeth and supports your immune system. Your skin makes it from **sunlight**, and you can also get it from oily fish, eggs and **fortified milk and cereals**."],
  ["what is rickets | what happens without vitamin d", "**Rickets** is a condition in children where bones become soft and can bend, caused mainly by too little **vitamin D**. It's rare today thanks to fortified foods and awareness, but it shows how important vitamin D is for growing bones."],
  ["what does vitamin e do | foods with vitamin e", "**Vitamin E** is an **antioxidant** that helps protect your cells from damage and supports your immune system and skin. Find it in **nuts, seeds** (especially sunflower seeds and almonds), vegetable oils and spinach."],
  ["what does vitamin k do | foods with vitamin k | why is vitamin k important", "**Vitamin K** helps your **blood clot** so cuts stop bleeding, and it supports strong bones. It's found in **leafy greens** like kale, spinach and broccoli. Your gut bacteria make a little too!"],
  ["what are minerals in food | what minerals does the body need | why do we need minerals", "**Minerals** are elements from the earth that your body needs, like **calcium** for bones, **iron** for blood, **potassium** for muscles and nerves, **zinc** for healing and **iodine** for the thyroid. Plants absorb them from soil, and we get them from food."],
  ["what does calcium do | foods with calcium | why do we need calcium", "**Calcium** builds strong **bones and teeth** and helps your muscles, nerves and heart work. Good sources include **milk, yogurt, cheese, fortified plant milks, tofu, almonds, broccoli and leafy greens**."],
  ["does milk make your bones strong | is milk good for you | do you need milk for strong bones", "Milk contains **calcium, protein and vitamin D**, which all help build bones. But you can also get these nutrients from other foods like fortified plant milks, tofu, beans and leafy greens. Exercise matters too!"],
  ["what does iron do | foods with iron | why do we need iron", "**Iron** helps your red blood cells carry **oxygen** around your body. Too little can make you tired. Find it in **red meat, chicken, fish, beans, lentils, spinach and fortified cereals**. Eating vitamin C foods alongside plant sources helps you absorb more."],
  ["what does potassium do | foods with potassium | are bananas high in potassium", "**Potassium** helps your **muscles, nerves and heart** work properly and balances fluids in your body. Bananas are a well-known source, but **potatoes, beans, spinach, avocados, oranges and yogurt** have plenty too."],
  ["what does magnesium do | foods with magnesium | why do we need magnesium", "**Magnesium** helps hundreds of body processes, including **muscle and nerve function**, energy production and bone health. Find it in **nuts, seeds, whole grains, beans, leafy greens** and dark chocolate."],
  ["what does zinc do | foods with zinc | why is zinc important", "**Zinc** helps your **immune system** fight germs, helps wounds heal and supports growth and your sense of taste and smell. Good sources are **meat, seafood, beans, nuts, seeds and whole grains**."],
  ["what does iodine do | why is salt iodized | foods with iodine", "**Iodine** is needed by your **thyroid** to make hormones that control metabolism and growth. Many countries add it to table salt (iodized salt) to prevent deficiency. It's also found in **seafood, dairy and eggs**."],
  ["what does sodium do | is salt bad for you | how much salt is too much", "Your body needs a little **sodium** for nerves, muscles and fluid balance, but most people eat **much more than they need**, mainly from processed and fast foods. Too much over time can raise blood pressure. Cooking at home and tasting before salting helps."],
  ["what does phosphorus do | foods with phosphorus", "**Phosphorus** works with calcium to build **bones and teeth** and helps your body make energy. It's found in **dairy, meat, fish, beans, nuts and whole grains**, so most people get enough."],
  ["what does fluoride do | why is fluoride in toothpaste | is fluoride good for teeth", "**Fluoride** is a mineral that **strengthens tooth enamel** and helps prevent cavities. It's in most toothpaste and in many places' tap water. Kids should use only a small amount of toothpaste and spit it out rather than swallow it."],
  ["what are antioxidants | what do antioxidants do | foods with antioxidants", "**Antioxidants** are substances that help protect your cells from damage by unstable molecules called free radicals. Colorful **fruits and vegetables**, berries, nuts, beans and whole grains are full of them."],
  ["what are the five food groups | what are the food groups | list the food groups", "The **five food groups** are:\n- **Fruits**\n- **Vegetables**\n- **Grains** (best as whole grains)\n- **Protein** foods (meat, fish, eggs, beans, nuts, tofu)\n- **Dairy** (or fortified alternatives)\nEating from all of them helps you get the full range of nutrients."],
  ["what is the food pyramid | is the food pyramid still used", "The **food pyramid** was an older diagram showing how much of each food group to eat, with grains at the bottom and fats and sweets at the top. Many countries now use **plate-shaped guides** instead, but the idea is the same: lots of plants, whole grains and variety."],
  ["why should i eat fruits and vegetables | why are vegetables good for you | benefits of fruits and veggies", "**Fruits and vegetables** are packed with vitamins, minerals, fiber, water and antioxidants. They help your body fight germs, keep digestion healthy, protect your heart and give you lasting energy. Aim to fill **half your plate** with them."],
  ["what does eat the rainbow mean | why eat different colored vegetables | colorful foods", "**Eating the rainbow** means choosing fruits and vegetables of many colors, like red tomatoes, orange carrots, yellow peppers, green spinach and purple berries. Different colors come from different nutrients, so variety gives you a wider mix."],
  ["how many fruits and vegetables should i eat a day | how many servings of vegetables | five a day", "Many health guides recommend at least **5 portions of fruits and vegetables a day**, and more is even better. A portion is roughly what fits in your hand, like an apple, a handful of berries or a few spoonfuls of veggies."],
  ["what are whole grains | whole grain vs refined | is brown rice better than white rice", "**Whole grains** keep all parts of the grain, including the fiber-rich bran and the nutrient-packed germ. **Refined grains** like white bread and white rice have those removed. Whole grains, like **brown rice, oats, whole-wheat bread and popcorn**, give more fiber and vitamins."],
  ["what are good sources of protein for vegetarians | vegetarian protein | plant based protein foods", "Great vegetarian protein sources include **beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, edamame, nuts, seeds, peanut butter, eggs, milk, yogurt and cheese**. Whole grains like quinoa and oats add some too."],
  ["what is a vegetarian | what is a vegan | vegetarian vs vegan", "A **vegetarian** doesn't eat meat or fish but may eat eggs and dairy. A **vegan** eats no animal products at all, including eggs, dairy and honey. Both can be healthy with good planning, especially for nutrients like **B12, iron and calcium**."],
  ["are eggs healthy | are eggs good for you | what nutrients are in eggs", "Eggs are a nutritious food with **high-quality protein**, vitamins like **B12 and D**, and choline for the brain. For most people they fit well into a balanced diet."],
  ["is fruit juice healthy | is juice as good as fruit | juice vs whole fruit", "**Whole fruit is better than juice**. Juice loses most of the fiber and has a lot of natural sugar in a quick gulp. If you drink juice, keep it to a small glass and choose 100% juice. Water and milk are better everyday drinks."],
  ["natural sugar vs added sugar | what is added sugar | is sugar in fruit bad", "**Natural sugars** are found in whole fruit and milk, which also give you fiber, vitamins and protein. **Added sugars** are put into foods like candy, soda, cookies and sweetened cereals. It's the **added sugars** that are worth limiting."],
  ["what is processed food | is processed food bad | what are ultra processed foods", "**Processed food** is anything changed from its natural form, which can be fine, like frozen veggies or plain yogurt. **Ultra-processed** foods, like many chips, sweets and sugary drinks, often have lots of added sugar, salt and fat and little fiber, so it's best to eat them only sometimes."],
  ["is fast food bad for you | is junk food unhealthy | can i eat junk food", "Fast food and junk food are often high in **salt, sugar and unhealthy fats** and low in fiber and vitamins. Enjoying them **once in a while** is fine; the problem is when they replace healthy meals regularly."],
  ["is chocolate healthy | is dark chocolate good for you", "**Dark chocolate** has some antioxidants and minerals, but chocolate also has **sugar and fat**, so it's best as an occasional treat. Milk chocolate has more sugar and less cocoa than dark."],
  ["what are superfoods | are superfoods real | do superfoods exist", "**'Superfood'** is a marketing word, not a science term. Foods like berries, kale and salmon are very nutritious, but **no single food** is magic. A varied diet with lots of plants is what really counts."],
  ["how do i read a nutrition label | how to read food labels | what does a nutrition facts label mean", "On a **nutrition label**, look at:\n- **Serving size** first, since all numbers are per serving\n- **Calories** for energy\n- **Fiber and protein**: more is usually better\n- **Added sugars, sodium and saturated fat**: less is usually better\n- **% Daily Value**: 5% or less is low, 20% or more is high"],
  ["what is a serving size | serving size vs portion size | how big is a serving", "A **serving size** is the standard amount listed on a food label. A **portion** is how much you actually put on your plate, which may be more or less. Your hand is a handy guide: a palm of protein, a fist of grains, a thumb of fat."],
  ["what are probiotics | what are prebiotics | are probiotics good for you", "**Probiotics** are live helpful bacteria found in foods like **yogurt, kefir and some pickled foods**. **Prebiotics** are fibers that feed your good gut bacteria, found in bananas, onions, garlic, oats and beans. Both can support a healthy gut."],
  ["is caffeine bad for kids | can kids drink coffee | how much caffeine is ok for kids", "Caffeine can make kids **jittery, anxious and have trouble sleeping**, and many health experts recommend kids avoid or strictly limit it. It's found in coffee, tea, many sodas, energy drinks and chocolate. Water and milk are better choices."],
  ["are energy drinks bad for kids | can kids have energy drinks | are energy drinks safe", "**Energy drinks aren't recommended for kids and teens**. They can contain lots of **caffeine and sugar**, which may cause a racing heart, anxiety, poor sleep and upset stomach. Water is the best way to stay energized."],
  ["what are electrolytes | do i need sports drinks | sports drinks vs water", "**Electrolytes** are minerals like sodium and potassium that help nerves, muscles and fluid balance. For everyday activity, **water is all you need**. Sports drinks may help during long, intense exercise in the heat, but they also contain sugar."],
  ["what are the signs of dehydration | how do i know if i'm dehydrated | what is dehydration", "**Dehydration** means your body doesn't have enough water. Signs include **thirst, dark yellow pee, a dry mouth, headache, tiredness and dizziness**. Drink water and rest in a cool place. Severe signs like confusion or fainting need medical help right away."],
  ["can you drink too much water | is too much water bad | what is water intoxication", "It's rare, but yes. Drinking a **huge amount of water very quickly** can dilute the salt in your blood, which is dangerous. For everyday life, just drink when thirsty and sip regularly; your kidneys handle normal amounts easily."],
  ["does food have water in it | which foods have the most water | can you get water from food", "Yes! About **20% of the water** people take in comes from food. Fruits and vegetables like **watermelon, cucumber, oranges, strawberries, lettuce and soup** are mostly water."],
  ["why is water better than soda | is soda bad for you | why should i drink water instead of soda", "**Water hydrates you with zero sugar**. Soda usually has lots of added sugar, which can harm teeth and adds empty calories, and some sodas contain caffeine. Water is the best everyday drink."],
  ["how much water should i drink when exercising | staying hydrated during sports", "Drink water **before, during and after** exercise. Take sips every 15 to 20 minutes when you're active, and more when it's hot or you're sweating a lot. Pale yellow pee is a good sign you're hydrated."],
  ["does sugar make kids hyper | is the sugar rush real | does candy make you hyper", "Studies have found that **sugar doesn't actually cause hyperactivity** in most kids. The excitement often comes from the situation, like a party. Still, too much added sugar isn't great for teeth or overall health."],
  ["should you starve a fever | feed a cold starve a fever | should i eat when i'm sick", "'Feed a cold, starve a fever' is a **myth**. When you're sick, your body needs **fluids and nutrition** to fight infection. Drink plenty and eat light, easy foods like soup, toast or fruit when you can."],
  ["what should i eat before sports | what to eat before exercise | pre workout snack for kids", "A couple of hours before sports, eat a meal with **carbs and some protein**, like a sandwich or pasta. Closer to playing, a light snack such as a **banana, toast or yogurt** works well. And don't forget water!"],
  ["what should i eat after exercise | recovery food after sports | post workout snack", "After exercise, your body likes **carbs to refuel** and **protein to repair muscles**, plus water. Easy options are chocolate milk, a yogurt with fruit, a turkey sandwich or eggs on toast."],
  ["are nuts healthy | why are nuts good for you | what nutrients are in nuts", "Nuts are packed with **healthy fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and magnesium**. A small handful makes a great snack. Some people have **nut allergies**, which can be serious, so always check before sharing."],
  ["are beans healthy | why are beans good for you | what nutrients are in beans", "Beans and lentils are nutrition superstars: full of **protein, fiber, iron, folate and potassium**, and low in fat. They're great for your heart and gut, and they're affordable too."],
  ["are potatoes healthy | are potatoes a vegetable | are french fries a vegetable", "**Potatoes** are a starchy vegetable with vitamin C, potassium and fiber (especially with the skin). Baked or boiled is healthiest; **french fries and chips** are fried, so they're more of an occasional treat."],
  ["is fruit a carb | does fruit have sugar | is it ok to eat lots of fruit", "Yes, fruit is mostly **carbohydrate**, including natural sugar, but it also comes with fiber, water, vitamins and antioxidants. Eating whole fruit every day is a healthy habit."],
  ["what is cholesterol in food | what foods have cholesterol", "**Dietary cholesterol** is found only in **animal foods**, like eggs, meat and dairy. For most people, saturated and trans fats affect blood cholesterol more than cholesterol in food does. Plants contain no cholesterol."],
  ["are vitamins in pills as good as food | do kids need vitamin supplements | should i take vitamins", "Most healthy kids can get the nutrients they need from a **varied diet**. Food also gives fiber and other helpful substances pills don't have. Some people do need supplements, but that's a decision for a **doctor or parent**, and you should never take them on your own."],
  ["what are healthy habits | list of healthy habits | how to be healthy every day", "Great everyday **healthy habits**:\n- Eat plenty of fruits, vegetables and whole grains\n- Drink water\n- Move your body for at least an hour\n- Get enough sleep\n- Wash your hands and brush your teeth\n- Take screen breaks\n- Talk about your feelings with people you trust"],
  ["how do i wash my hands properly | steps for handwashing | how long should you wash your hands", "Proper handwashing:\n1. Wet your hands with clean water\n2. Add soap and lather the backs, between fingers and under nails\n3. Scrub for at least **20 seconds** (hum 'Happy Birthday' twice)\n4. Rinse well\n5. Dry with a clean towel or air dryer"],
  ["when should i wash my hands | when to wash hands | times you should wash your hands", "Wash your hands:\n- Before eating or preparing food\n- After using the toilet\n- After blowing your nose, coughing or sneezing\n- After touching animals or garbage\n- After playing outside\n- When you get home from school or public places"],
  ["is hand sanitizer as good as soap | hand sanitizer vs soap and water | does hand sanitizer work", "**Soap and water is best**, especially when hands are visibly dirty. When soap isn't available, an **alcohol-based hand sanitizer** (at least 60% alcohol) is a good backup. Rub it all over your hands until dry."],
  ["why should i cover my cough | how to cough properly | why sneeze into your elbow", "Coughs and sneezes spray tiny **droplets full of germs** that can travel a few meters. Covering with a **tissue or your elbow** keeps them from reaching others or getting on your hands, which then touch everything."],
  ["why shouldn't i share cups | is it bad to share drinks | can you get sick from sharing food", "Sharing cups, straws, utensils or bottles can pass **germs in saliva** from one person to another, spreading colds, flu and other illnesses. It's safer to use your own."],
  ["how often should i shower | how often should kids bathe | do i need to shower every day", "Younger kids often only need a bath **a few times a week**, unless they're dirty or sweaty. Once **puberty** starts, a **daily shower** usually helps with sweat and body odor. Always wash after swimming or sports."],
  ["what is good hygiene | personal hygiene tips | how to have good hygiene", "Good **personal hygiene** means:\n- Washing your hands often\n- Showering or bathing regularly\n- Brushing teeth twice a day and flossing\n- Wearing clean clothes and socks\n- Keeping nails trimmed\n- Washing your hair regularly"],
  ["do i need deodorant | when should kids start wearing deodorant | deodorant vs antiperspirant", "Many kids start using **deodorant during puberty**, when sweat glands in the armpits become more active. **Deodorant** covers or reduces odor, while **antiperspirant** reduces sweating. Showering regularly matters most."],
  ["what are the types of exercise | different kinds of exercise | four types of exercise", "There are **4 main types of exercise**:\n- **Aerobic (endurance)**: running, swimming, biking, dancing\n- **Strength**: push-ups, climbing, lifting\n- **Flexibility**: stretching, yoga\n- **Balance**: standing on one foot, gymnastics, tai chi"],
  ["what is aerobic exercise | what is cardio | examples of cardio", "**Aerobic exercise (cardio)** is activity that gets your heart pumping and makes you breathe harder for a while, like **running, swimming, biking, jumping rope or dancing**. It strengthens your heart and lungs."],
  ["what is anaerobic exercise | aerobic vs anaerobic | what is sprinting good for", "**Anaerobic exercise** is short, intense bursts, like sprinting or jumping, where your muscles work so hard they briefly use energy without enough oxygen. **Aerobic exercise** is steady and longer. Both are good, and they build different kinds of fitness."],
  ["is strength training safe for kids | can kids lift weights | strength exercises for kids", "**Strength training can be safe for kids** when it's supervised and uses proper form. Bodyweight moves like **push-ups, squats, planks and climbing** are great. Heavy lifting should be guided by a trained adult."],
  ["how much exercise do kids need | how long should kids exercise each day | how much physical activity for children", "Health experts recommend kids and teens get at least **60 minutes of activity every day**, mostly aerobic, plus muscle- and bone-strengthening activities like climbing and jumping at least **3 days a week**."],
  ["how much exercise do adults need | how many minutes of exercise per week for adults", "Adults are advised to get at least **150 minutes of moderate activity a week** (like brisk walking), or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, plus **strength exercises 2 days a week**."],
  ["how does exercise help your brain | does exercise make you smarter | exercise and mood", "Exercise boosts blood flow to your brain, helps you **focus and remember**, improves sleep and releases chemicals like **endorphins** that lift your mood and reduce stress."],
  ["what are endorphins | what is runner's high | why do i feel happy after exercise", "**Endorphins** are natural chemicals your brain releases during exercise, laughter and other activities. They reduce pain and stress and can make you feel **happy and relaxed**, sometimes called a 'runner's high'."],
  ["why should i cool down after exercise | what is a cool down | how to cool down after a workout", "A **cool down** of slow walking and gentle stretches for 5 to 10 minutes lets your heart rate and breathing return to normal gradually and can help you feel less stiff and dizzy afterward."],
  ["what is endurance | how to build stamina | how do i get better at running long distances", "**Endurance (stamina)** is how long you can keep going. Build it by doing aerobic activity regularly and slowly increasing time or distance, about 10% a week. Rest days help your body adapt."],
  ["what is flexibility | why is flexibility important | how to become more flexible", "**Flexibility** is how far your joints and muscles can move. It helps you move easily and may reduce injury. Gentle stretching after you're warmed up, held for about **15 to 30 seconds** without bouncing, improves it over time."],
  ["what are the benefits of yoga | is yoga good for kids | what does yoga do", "**Yoga** builds **flexibility, strength and balance** and uses slow breathing that helps you relax and focus. It's a gentle activity that kids and adults can enjoy."],
  ["is walking good exercise | benefits of walking | how many steps should i walk a day", "**Walking is great exercise**! It strengthens your heart, bones and muscles and boosts your mood. A brisk walk counts as moderate activity. There's no magic step number, but more movement throughout the day is better."],
  ["why are team sports good | benefits of playing sports | should i join a sports team", "**Team sports** build fitness, coordination and skills, and also teach **teamwork, communication and handling wins and losses**. Plus, they're a fun way to make friends."],
  ["should i exercise when i'm sick | is it ok to work out with a cold", "If your symptoms are mild and **above the neck** (like a stuffy nose), gentle activity may be okay. If you have a **fever, body aches, or a chest or stomach illness**, rest until you're better. When unsure, ask an adult or doctor."],
  ["what is a sedentary lifestyle | is sitting too much bad | why shouldn't i sit all day", "A **sedentary lifestyle** means sitting or lying down for most of the day with little activity. Too much sitting is linked to weaker muscles and heart health problems. Take breaks to stand, stretch or walk at least **every hour**."],
  ["what is a target heart rate | what heart rate should i exercise at | how hard should i exercise", "An easy way to judge effort is the **talk test**: during moderate exercise you can talk but not sing; during vigorous exercise you can only say a few words. Heart-rate targets vary by age and fitness."],
  ["what is good posture | how should i sit | how to stand up straight", "**Good posture** means your ears, shoulders and hips line up. When sitting: **feet flat on the floor, back supported, shoulders relaxed**, and screen at eye level. When standing: stand tall, shoulders back but relaxed, weight on both feet."],
  ["why is good posture important | does bad posture hurt you | what happens with bad posture", "Good posture puts less strain on your **muscles, joints and spine**, helps you breathe more easily and can prevent back and neck aches. Moving often matters too: no posture is good if you hold it for hours."],
  ["what is text neck | does looking down at your phone hurt your neck | phone neck pain", "**Text neck** is neck and shoulder strain from looking down at a phone or tablet for a long time. Your head gets 'heavier' for your neck the more it tilts forward. Hold devices closer to **eye level** and take breaks."],
  ["how should i set up my computer desk | ergonomic setup for kids | how to sit at a computer", "A comfy computer setup:\n- Top of the screen at or slightly below **eye level**\n- Screen about an **arm's length** away\n- Feet flat (use a footrest if needed)\n- Elbows bent about 90 degrees\n- Wrists straight while typing"],
  ["how should i carry a backpack | how heavy should a backpack be | backpack back pain", "Wear your backpack on **both shoulders**, tighten the straps so it sits close to your back, and use the waist strap if it has one. Experts often suggest it weigh **no more than 10 to 15%** of your body weight. Put heavier items closest to your back."],
  ["what is the 20 20 20 rule | how to rest your eyes from screens | eye breaks for screen time", "The **20-20-20 rule**: every **20 minutes**, look at something **20 feet (6 meters) away** for **20 seconds**. It relaxes your eye muscles and helps prevent eye strain."],
  ["does screen time hurt your eyes | what is digital eye strain | why do my eyes hurt after using the computer", "Long screen time can cause **digital eye strain**: tired, dry or sore eyes, blurry vision and headaches. It's usually temporary. Take breaks, blink often, keep screens at arm's length and adjust brightness to match the room. Spending time outdoors is good for kids' eyes too."],
  ["does blue light affect sleep | should i use my phone before bed | screens before bedtime", "Light from screens, especially **blue light**, can delay **melatonin**, the sleep hormone, and exciting content keeps your brain alert. Try to turn off screens about **an hour before bed** and use night mode in the evening."],
  ["how much screen time is ok for kids | how much screen time should i have | screen time limits", "There's no single perfect number, but experts agree screen time shouldn't crowd out **sleep, exercise, homework, family time and face-to-face fun**. Many guidelines suggest around **1 hour a day** of quality content for young children. Older kids should work out sensible limits with their parents."],
  ["why are my eyes dry when using screens | dry eyes from computer | why do my eyes feel tired from screens", "When you focus on a screen, you **blink much less** than usual, so your eyes dry out. Make a habit of blinking fully, following the **20-20-20 rule**, and keeping the screen slightly below eye level."],
  ["does sitting close to the tv ruin your eyes | is it bad to sit close to the screen", "Sitting close to the TV **won't permanently damage your eyes**, though it can cause temporary strain. If a child always needs to sit close to see clearly, it might be a sign they need an **eye exam**."],
  ["does reading in the dark hurt your eyes | is reading in dim light bad", "Reading in dim light can make your eyes **tired and strained**, but it **doesn't cause permanent damage**. Good lighting just makes reading more comfortable."],
  ["is spending time outside good for your eyes | does going outside prevent nearsightedness", "Research suggests kids who spend more **time outdoors** in daylight are less likely to become nearsighted. Playing outside is great for your eyes, body and mood. Remember sunglasses and sunscreen on bright days."],
  ["how do germs spread | how do people catch germs | ways germs spread", "Germs spread through:\n- **Droplets** from coughs and sneezes\n- **Touching** people or surfaces, then your eyes, nose or mouth\n- **Contaminated food or water**\n- **Bug bites** for some diseases\nHandwashing is one of the best ways to stop them."],
  ["what are the types of germs | what kinds of germs are there | bacteria viruses fungi parasites", "The four main types of germs are:\n- **Bacteria**: tiny single cells, some helpful and some harmful\n- **Viruses**: much smaller, can only multiply inside living cells\n- **Fungi**: like molds and yeasts\n- **Parasites**: like worms or lice that live on or in another living thing"],
  ["what is athlete's foot | can fungus make you sick | what are fungal infections", "Most fungi are harmless, but some can cause skin infections like **athlete's foot**, which makes skin between the toes itchy and flaky. Keeping feet clean and dry and wearing flip-flops in shared showers helps prevent it."],
  ["what are parasites | what is a parasite in the body", "A **parasite** is a living thing that lives on or inside another living thing and takes food from it. In people, examples include **head lice** and some kinds of intestinal worms. Good hygiene, clean water and cooked food help prevent them."],
  ["how big is a virus compared to bacteria | how small are germs | can you see germs", "Germs are far too small to see without a **microscope**. Bacteria are about **1 to 2 micrometers** long, and most viruses are **10 to 100 times smaller** than that. Thousands of bacteria could fit across the width of a hair."],
  ["what is a pathogen | are all germs pathogens", "A **pathogen** is any germ that can cause disease. Most microbes are **not** pathogens; many are harmless or even helpful, like the bacteria in your gut."],
  ["how long do germs live on surfaces | how long do viruses survive on things", "It depends on the germ and the surface. Some viruses survive only **minutes to hours**, while others can last **days** on hard surfaces like doorknobs and phones. That's why cleaning shared surfaces and washing your hands matter."],
  ["is the five second rule true | is it ok to eat food off the floor | 5 second rule", "The **five-second rule is a myth**. Germs can stick to food **instantly**, especially wet food. How dirty the floor is matters more than how fast you pick it up. When in doubt, throw it out."],
  ["why do people get sick more in winter | why are colds more common in winter | does cold weather make you sick", "Cold weather itself doesn't give you a cold, **viruses** do. But in winter people spend more time **indoors close together**, and some viruses survive better in cold, dry air, so they spread more easily."],
  ["can you catch a cold from wet hair | does going outside with wet hair make you sick | can you catch a cold from being cold | does being cold make you catch a cold", "**No**, wet hair or being cold doesn't directly cause a cold. Colds are caused by **viruses** you catch from other people. You might feel chilly, but you won't get sick from that alone."],
  ["what is cpr | how does cpr work | can kids learn cpr", "**CPR** (cardiopulmonary resuscitation) is an emergency technique that uses **chest compressions** to keep blood flowing when someone's heart has stopped. Many schools and organizations teach it; the best way to learn is a **hands-on class**. Always call emergency services first."],
  ["what is heatstroke | what is heat exhaustion | heat exhaustion vs heatstroke", "**Heat exhaustion** causes heavy sweating, weakness, dizziness and headache: move to shade, drink water and cool down. **Heatstroke** is more serious, with very high body temperature and confusion: it's an **emergency**. Stay safe by drinking water, resting in shade and avoiding the hottest part of the day."],
  ["what is hypothermia | what is frostbite | what happens when your body gets too cold", "**Hypothermia** is when your body gets dangerously cold, causing shivering, confusion and sleepiness. **Frostbite** is when skin and tissue freeze, usually fingers, toes, ears and nose. Dress in layers, cover your skin and come inside if you're very cold. Both need **medical help**."],
  ["what is the common cold | what causes a cold | cold symptoms", "The **common cold** is a mild infection of your nose and throat caused by viruses, most often **rhinoviruses**. Symptoms include a runny or stuffy nose, sneezing, sore throat and cough. It usually gets better in **7 to 10 days** with rest and fluids. See a doctor if you have trouble breathing, a high fever or you're getting worse."],
  ["how long does a cold last | how to get over a cold faster | what helps a cold", "Most colds last about **a week to 10 days**. There's no cure, but **rest, fluids, warm soup** and honey in warm water (not for babies under 1) can help you feel better. Ask an adult or doctor before taking any medicine."],
  ["what is the flu | what is influenza | flu symptoms", "The **flu (influenza)** is caused by influenza viruses. It often hits suddenly with **fever, body aches, chills, tiredness, cough and sore throat**, and can make you feel much worse than a cold. Rest and fluids help, and you should see a doctor if you're very sick or have trouble breathing. A yearly flu vaccine helps protect people."],
  ["cold vs flu | what is the difference between a cold and the flu | is it a cold or the flu", "A **cold** comes on gradually and mostly affects your nose and throat. The **flu** usually starts **suddenly** with fever, body aches and exhaustion, and can be more serious. Only a doctor can tell for sure, so check with one if you're very sick."],
  ["why do we need a flu shot every year | why is the flu vaccine yearly", "Flu viruses **change a little every year**, and protection from the vaccine fades over time. Scientists update the flu vaccine each year to match the strains expected to spread."],
  ["what causes a sore throat | why does my throat hurt | what is strep throat", "Most **sore throats** are caused by viruses like colds. **Strep throat** is caused by bacteria and often comes with a fever and very painful swallowing, without much cough. A doctor can test for strep, so see one if your sore throat is severe or comes with a fever."],
  ["what are allergies | why do people have allergies | what causes allergies", "An **allergy** is when your immune system **overreacts** to something harmless, like pollen, dust mites, pet dander or certain foods, treating it like an invader. That causes symptoms like sneezing, itchy eyes, rashes or tummy trouble. A doctor can help figure out what you're allergic to."],
  ["what is hay fever | what is a pollen allergy | why do i sneeze in spring", "**Hay fever** is an allergy to **pollen** from trees, grass or weeds, and it has nothing to do with hay or fever! It causes sneezing, a runny nose and itchy, watery eyes, often in spring and summer. Keeping windows closed on high-pollen days helps, and a doctor can suggest treatment."],
  ["why am i allergic to cats | what is a pet allergy | are some dogs hypoallergenic", "Pet allergies are usually caused by proteins in an animal's **skin flakes (dander), saliva and urine**, not the fur itself. No dog or cat is completely allergy-free, though some people react less to certain breeds."],
  ["what is a peanut allergy | what is anaphylaxis | severe allergic reaction signs", "A **severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis)** can cause trouble breathing, swelling of the face or throat, hives, vomiting or fainting. It's an **emergency**: call emergency services right away, and if the person has a prescribed **epinephrine auto-injector**, an adult should help them use it. Peanuts, tree nuts, shellfish and bee stings are common triggers."],
  ["what is asthma | what causes asthma | asthma symptoms", "**Asthma** is a condition where the airways in the lungs get **swollen and narrow**, making it hard to breathe. Signs include wheezing, coughing, chest tightness and shortness of breath. Triggers can include colds, exercise, smoke, pollen and cold air. A doctor creates an **asthma action plan** to manage it."],
  ["what is an asthma attack | what to do during an asthma attack | how to help someone with asthma", "During an **asthma attack**, the airways tighten and breathing gets hard. Help the person **sit upright, stay calm and use their prescribed inhaler** following their asthma plan, and get an adult. If they're struggling to breathe, can't talk or lips turn blue, **call emergency services**."],
  ["what is an inhaler | how does an inhaler work | what is a spacer", "An **inhaler** is a small device that delivers medicine straight into the lungs as a mist or powder. Some open the airways quickly during symptoms, and others reduce swelling over time. A **spacer** is a tube that helps get more medicine into the lungs. A doctor decides which inhaler and how to use it."],
  ["can kids with asthma play sports | is exercise ok with asthma", "Yes! Many top athletes have asthma. With a good **asthma plan from a doctor**, warming up properly and having their inhaler nearby, most kids with asthma can play sports and stay active."],
  ["what is chickenpox | chickenpox symptoms | how do you get chickenpox", "**Chickenpox** is a contagious illness caused by the **varicella-zoster virus**. It causes a fever and an itchy rash of small blisters all over the body. Try not to scratch, to avoid scars and infection. There's a **vaccine** that prevents most cases. See a doctor to confirm it and for advice."],
  ["can you get chickenpox twice | what is shingles | how is shingles related to chickenpox", "Most people only get chickenpox **once**. But the virus can stay **hidden in nerve cells** and wake up years later as **shingles**, a painful, blistering rash usually on one side of the body. It mostly affects older adults, and there's a vaccine for it too."],
  ["what is measles | why is measles dangerous | measles symptoms", "**Measles** is a very contagious virus that causes high fever, cough, runny nose, red eyes and a rash that spreads from the face downward. It can cause serious complications, especially in young children. The **MMR vaccine** protects against measles, mumps and rubella."],
  ["what is an ear infection | why does my ear hurt | ear infection symptoms", "An **ear infection** usually happens in the middle ear behind the eardrum, often after a cold, when fluid gets trapped and germs grow. It can cause **ear pain, fever and trouble hearing**. They're common in young kids. See a doctor if ear pain is bad, lasts more than a day or two, or there's fluid draining."],
  ["what is pink eye | what is conjunctivitis | is pink eye contagious", "**Pink eye (conjunctivitis)** is swelling of the thin layer covering the white of the eye, making it **pink, itchy or gritty**, sometimes with discharge. It can be caused by viruses, bacteria or allergies, and some types spread easily. Don't share towels, wash your hands, and see a doctor."],
  ["what is a stomach bug | what is gastroenteritis | stomach flu symptoms", "A **stomach bug (gastroenteritis)** is an infection of the gut, usually from a virus, causing vomiting, diarrhea and tummy cramps. It's not actually the flu. The most important thing is to **keep sipping fluids** to avoid dehydration. See a doctor if you can't keep fluids down, there's blood, or signs of dehydration."],
  ["what is head lice | how do you get lice | how to get rid of lice", "**Head lice** are tiny insects that live in hair and cause itching. They spread by **head-to-head contact** and sharing hats or brushes, not because someone is dirty. Special combs and treatments get rid of them; ask a parent or doctor for help."],
  ["what is a rash | why do i have a rash | what causes skin rashes", "A **rash** is an area of red, bumpy, itchy or irritated skin. Causes include allergies, heat, viruses, insect bites and things that irritate skin. Tell an adult about any rash, and get medical help quickly if it comes with a fever, spreads fast, or doesn't fade when you press a glass against it."],
  ["when should i see a doctor | when should i go to the doctor | how do i know if i need a doctor", "Tell a parent or trusted adult and see a doctor if:\n- You have a high fever or one lasting more than a few days\n- You have trouble breathing\n- Pain is severe or won't go away\n- You can't keep fluids down\n- You're getting worse instead of better\nFor emergencies, call your local emergency number."],
  ["what happens at a checkup | why go to the doctor when you're not sick | what is a physical exam", "At a **checkup**, the doctor measures your **height and weight**, checks your heart, lungs, ears, eyes and throat, asks how you're feeling, and makes sure you're up to date on vaccines. It's a great time to ask questions about your health."],
  ["what is a stethoscope | what does a doctor listen to with a stethoscope", "A **stethoscope** lets doctors and nurses listen to sounds inside your body, like your **heartbeat, breathing and stomach noises**. Those sounds help them check that everything is working normally."],
  ["what kinds of doctors are there | what is a pediatrician | what does a dermatologist do", "Some types of doctors:\n- **Pediatrician**: children's doctor\n- **Dermatologist**: skin, hair and nails\n- **Ophthalmologist**: eye doctor (optometrists check vision too)\n- **Cardiologist**: heart\n- **Orthopedist**: bones and joints\n- **Dentist** and **orthodontist**: teeth and braces"],
  ["what is an mri | how does an mri work | is an mri scary", "An **MRI** uses a strong **magnet and radio waves** (not X-rays) to take detailed pictures of the inside of your body. You lie still inside a tube-shaped machine that makes loud knocking sounds. It doesn't hurt, and you're often given earplugs or music."],
  ["what is a blood test | why do doctors take blood | does a blood test hurt", "A **blood test** takes a small sample of blood, usually from your arm or finger, to check things like blood cells, sugar and vitamins. It feels like a quick pinch. Looking away and slow breathing can help if you're nervous."],
  ["what are the types of teeth | what are incisors canines and molars | what do different teeth do", "You have 4 types of teeth:\n- **Incisors** (front): cut and bite\n- **Canines** (pointy): tear food\n- **Premolars**: crush and grind\n- **Molars** (back): chew and grind"],
  ["when do baby teeth fall out | at what age do you lose your first tooth | how many baby teeth do kids have", "Kids have **20 baby teeth**. They usually start falling out around **age 6**, often the front teeth first, and the last ones go by about age 12. Adult teeth push up from underneath and replace them."],
  ["what is tooth enamel | can enamel grow back | what protects your teeth", "**Enamel** is the hard, shiny outer layer of your teeth and the **hardest substance in your body**. It **can't grow back** once it's lost, so protect it by brushing with fluoride toothpaste and limiting sugary and acidic drinks."],
  ["what is plaque | what is tartar | plaque vs tartar", "**Plaque** is a sticky film of bacteria that forms on teeth every day. The bacteria feed on sugar and make acid that causes cavities. If plaque isn't brushed off, it hardens into **tartar**, which only a dental hygienist can remove."],
  ["why should i floss | how often should i floss | is flossing important", "**Flossing** cleans between your teeth and under the gumline where a toothbrush can't reach, removing plaque and food that cause cavities and gum disease. Dentists recommend flossing **once a day**."],
  ["how often should i go to the dentist | why go to the dentist | what happens at the dentist", "Most people should visit the dentist about **every 6 months**, or as often as the dentist suggests. They clean your teeth, check for cavities and gum problems, and may take X-rays."],
  ["why do people get braces | what do braces do | how do braces work", "**Braces** use brackets and wires (or clear aligners) to gently push teeth into better positions over time. They fix crooked or crowded teeth and bite problems, making teeth easier to clean and chew with. An **orthodontist** puts them on."],
  ["what are wisdom teeth | why do people get wisdom teeth removed", "**Wisdom teeth** are the last molars at the very back, usually coming in between ages **17 and 25**. Many jaws don't have room for them, so they can get stuck or crowd other teeth, and a dentist may recommend removing them."],
  ["why are my teeth sensitive | why do my teeth hurt with cold drinks", "Teeth can feel **sensitive** when enamel wears down or gums pull back, exposing a softer layer with tiny tubes leading to the nerve. Cavities can also cause it. Tell an adult and see a dentist if it keeps happening."],
  ["what foods are good for your teeth | what foods are bad for your teeth | healthy snacks for teeth", "Good for teeth: **water, milk, cheese, yogurt, crunchy vegetables and fruits, and nuts**. Hard on teeth: **sticky candy, sugary drinks and frequent snacking on sweets**, because mouth bacteria turn sugar into acid that attacks enamel. Rinsing with water after sweets helps."],
  ["what is gum disease | why do my gums bleed when i brush | what is gingivitis", "**Gingivitis** is early gum disease caused by plaque along the gumline, making gums **red, puffy and bleed easily**. Brushing twice a day and flossing daily usually fixes it. If bleeding continues, see a dentist."],
  ["what is a mouthguard | do i need a mouthguard for sports", "A **mouthguard** is a soft plastic shield worn over your teeth to protect them during sports like hockey, football, basketball and martial arts. It helps prevent chipped or knocked-out teeth."],
  ["what should i do if a tooth gets knocked out | knocked out tooth first aid", "If an **adult tooth** gets knocked out, pick it up by the **crown (top), not the root**, rinse gently if dirty, and try to put it back in the socket or keep it in **milk**. Get to a dentist **right away**, ideally within an hour."],
  ["what is puberty | what happens during puberty | why do we go through puberty", "**Puberty** is the time when a child's body gradually develops into an adult body, guided by **hormones**. Changes include growth spurts, new body hair, more sweat and oil, voice changes, and body shape changes. It's a normal part of growing up that everyone goes through."],
  ["when does puberty start | what age does puberty begin | is it normal to start puberty early or late", "Puberty usually starts between about **8 and 13 for girls** and **9 and 14 for boys**, and everyone has their own timing. Starting a bit earlier or later than friends is usually normal. If you're worried, talk to a parent or doctor."],
  ["what is a growth spurt | why do teens grow so fast | when do growth spurts happen", "A **growth spurt** is a period of fast growth during puberty, when you might grow several centimeters in a year. Feet and hands often grow first. You may feel hungrier and more tired, so good food and sleep help."],
  ["when do you stop growing | at what age do people stop getting taller", "Most people stop growing taller when their **growth plates close** after puberty, usually around **14 to 16 for girls** and **16 to 18 for boys**, though it varies. Genes play the biggest role in your final height."],
  ["why does a boy's voice crack | why do voices get deeper in puberty | why does my voice crack", "During puberty, the **voice box grows** and the vocal cords get longer and thicker, which makes the voice deeper. While the muscles adjust, the voice can **crack or squeak** for a while. Everyone's voice changes some, but it's usually more noticeable in boys."],
  ["what is a period | what is menstruation | why do girls get periods", "A **period (menstruation)** is a normal part of puberty for girls. About once a month, the lining of the uterus, which gets ready in case of a pregnancy, isn't needed and leaves the body as blood through the vagina over a few days. It's healthy and natural; a parent, school nurse or doctor can answer questions and help with supplies."],
  ["why do teenagers need more sleep | why are teens tired all the time | why do teens stay up late", "During puberty, the body clock **shifts later**, so teens naturally feel sleepy later at night, while growth and brain development mean they need about **8 to 10 hours** of sleep. A regular schedule and screen-free time before bed help."],
  ["is it normal to grow at different rates | why am i shorter than my friends | why are some kids taller", "Yes, it's **completely normal**. Everyone starts puberty and growth spurts at different times, and genes play a big role. Kids who are shorter now often catch up later. If you're worried, a doctor can check your growth."],
  ["why do i feel moody during puberty | mood swings in puberty | why are teenagers emotional", "Changing **hormones** and a developing brain can make emotions feel stronger and shift quickly during puberty. That's normal. Getting enough sleep, exercise and talking to someone you trust can really help."],
  ["who can i talk to about puberty | i have questions about my body | who to ask about growing up", "It's completely okay to have questions! Good people to ask include a **parent or guardian, an older family member, a school nurse, a counselor or your doctor**. Trusted books written for kids about growing up can help too."],
  ["what is mental health | why is mental health important | what does mental health mean", "**Mental health** is about how we think, feel and handle life, just like physical health is about our bodies. Everyone has mental health, and it can go up and down. Taking care of it with sleep, exercise, connection and talking about feelings is just as important as caring for your body."],
  ["what is stress | why do we feel stressed | is stress bad", "**Stress** is your body's reaction to a challenge, like a test or a big game. A little stress can help you focus, but too much for too long can make you tired, worried or sick. Rest, exercise, breathing exercises and talking to someone help. If stress feels overwhelming, **talk to a trusted adult or professional**."],
  ["what is anxiety | what does anxiety feel like | anxiety vs worry", "**Anxiety** is a feeling of worry, nervousness or fear. It can cause a racing heart, a tight chest, an upset stomach or trouble sleeping. Some worry is normal, but if it happens a lot or gets in the way of daily life, **talk to a trusted adult, school counselor or doctor**: anxiety is very treatable."],
  ["what is a panic attack | what does a panic attack feel like | how to calm a panic attack", "A **panic attack** is a sudden wave of intense fear with physical symptoms like a pounding heart, fast breathing, shaking or dizziness. It feels scary but passes, usually within minutes. Slow breathing, like breathing in for 4 and out for 6, can help. If they keep happening, **talk to a trusted adult or doctor**."],
  ["what is depression | is sadness the same as depression | signs of depression", "Everyone feels **sad** sometimes. **Depression** is different: it's when sadness, emptiness or losing interest in things lasts for **weeks** and affects sleep, eating, school or friendships. It's a real health condition and it's treatable. **Talk to a trusted adult or a professional** if you feel this way."],
  ["what are emotions | why do we have feelings | what are the basic emotions", "**Emotions** are your brain and body's reactions to what happens around you, like happiness, sadness, anger, fear, surprise and disgust. They help you understand situations, connect with others and make decisions. All feelings are okay to have; what matters is how we handle them."],
  ["what is the 5 4 3 2 1 grounding technique | grounding exercise | how to calm down quickly", "The **5-4-3-2-1 grounding** trick brings your attention to the present:\n- **5** things you can see\n- **4** things you can touch\n- **3** things you can hear\n- **2** things you can smell\n- **1** thing you can taste\nIt helps calm racing thoughts."],
  ["what is mindfulness | how do you practice mindfulness | is meditation good for kids", "**Mindfulness** means paying attention to the present moment calmly and without judging. You can practice by focusing on your breathing, noticing sounds around you, or eating slowly and paying attention to the taste. Even a few minutes a day can help reduce stress."],
  ["what is self esteem | how can i feel better about myself | how to build confidence", "**Self-esteem** is how you feel about yourself. To build it, try new things, notice what you're good at, practice kind self-talk (talk to yourself like a good friend would), and spend time with people who support you. Everyone makes mistakes; they're part of learning."],
  ["how can i help a friend who is sad | my friend is upset | how to support a friend", "You can help by **listening** without judging, letting them know you care, and spending time with them. If your friend seems very sad for a long time, talks about hurting themselves or is in danger, **tell a trusted adult right away**, even if they asked you to keep it secret. That's being a good friend."],
  ["who can i talk to about my feelings | i need someone to talk to | is it ok to ask for help", "Asking for help is **brave and smart**. You can talk to a parent or guardian, another family member, a teacher, a school counselor, a coach or your doctor. Many countries also have free kids' helplines. If you're ever in danger, call your local emergency number."],
  ["what is therapy | what does a counselor do | what is a therapist", "A **therapist or counselor** is a trained professional who helps people talk through feelings, handle stress and learn coping skills. Going to therapy is like seeing a coach for your mind, and lots of people of all ages do it."],
  ["what is grief | how to deal with losing someone | my pet died", "**Grief** is the deep sadness we feel after losing someone or something we love, like a family member or a pet. It can come in waves and include sadness, anger or confusion. Talking about memories, crying and spending time with loved ones helps. Let a trusted adult know how you're feeling."],
  ["does sleep affect mood | why am i grumpy when tired | sleep and mental health", "Yes, a lot! When you don't sleep enough, your brain has a harder time **controlling emotions**, so you might feel grumpy, anxious or sad more easily. Good sleep helps you feel calmer and handle problems better."],
  ["how does journaling help | why write in a journal | is writing feelings down good", "**Journaling** helps you sort through thoughts and feelings, notice patterns and feel calmer. Writing down worries can make them feel smaller, and writing **things you're grateful for** can boost your mood."],
  ["do we swallow spiders in our sleep | is it true we eat spiders when sleeping", "That's a **myth**! Sleeping people make vibrations and noise (like breathing and heartbeats) that spiders tend to avoid. There's no evidence people swallow spiders regularly in their sleep."],
  ["does gum stay in your stomach for 7 years | what happens if you swallow gum", "**No**, swallowed gum doesn't stay in your stomach for 7 years. Your body can't digest the gum base, but it simply **passes through** your digestive system and comes out in a few days. Still, it's best not to swallow it."],
  ["do you lose most body heat through your head | should you wear a hat in winter", "It's a **myth** that you lose most of your heat through your head. You lose heat from **any uncovered part** of your body. But since your head is often the only uncovered part in winter, wearing a hat really does help."],
  ["does shaving make hair grow back thicker | does cutting hair make it grow faster", "**No**, shaving or cutting hair doesn't make it grow back thicker, darker or faster. Regrowing hair can feel coarse because it has a blunt tip instead of a tapered one."],
  ["is it bad to swim right after eating | do you have to wait 30 minutes to swim after eating", "There's **no good evidence** that swimming after eating is dangerous. A big meal might make you feel sluggish or crampy, so a light snack before swimming is more comfortable. Always swim with an adult around."],
  ["do we really need 8 glasses of water a day | is 8 glasses of water a myth", "The '8 glasses a day' rule is a **simple guide, not an exact science**. How much you need depends on your age, size, activity and the weather, and food and other drinks count too. Drink when thirsty and aim for **pale yellow pee**."],
  ["what is covid 19 | what is coronavirus | covid symptoms", "**COVID-19** is an illness caused by a coronavirus called SARS-CoV-2, which spreads mainly through the air when people breathe, talk, cough or sneeze. Symptoms can include fever, cough, sore throat, tiredness and loss of taste or smell. Vaccines, handwashing and staying home when sick help reduce spread. See a doctor if you're worried, and get emergency help for trouble breathing."],
  ["what is a seizure | what is epilepsy | what to do if someone has a seizure", "A **seizure** is a burst of unusual electrical activity in the brain that can cause shaking, staring or loss of awareness. **Epilepsy** is a condition with repeated seizures. If someone has one, **get an adult**, move hard objects away, cushion their head, never put anything in their mouth, and call emergency services if it lasts more than 5 minutes or they're hurt."],
  ["what is a nosebleed caused by | why do kids get nosebleeds | why does my nose bleed", "**Nosebleeds** are common in kids because the inside of the nose has many tiny blood vessels close to the surface. **Dry air, nose picking, colds, allergies or bumps** can break them. Using a humidifier and not picking help prevent them. Frequent or heavy nosebleeds should be checked by a doctor."],
  ["what is a pulled muscle | what does a pulled muscle feel like", "A **pulled muscle** is a muscle strain, when fibers get overstretched or slightly torn, often from sudden movements or skipping a warm-up. It feels sore, tight or weak. Rest and a wrapped cold pack help; see a doctor if pain is severe or you can't move normally."]
);
